/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:53:* ''The persona root'' — the face's own operator key, its op-key under the face's persona KEL. It signs the root half of each device delegation and nothing on a device's behalf; recovery rotates it beneath the same KEL prefix through the face's guardians, so continuity rides the log, never a frozen key.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:54:* ''The face atom'' — the key ONE device meets ONE face through. It mints at random when that device founds or joins the face, derives from no other seed (not the place key, not the persona root, not a sibling face's atom), and rests as a D row in that face's own custody on that device, keyed by its PersonaGroup (`face-atom-g<group digest>.bin` on the node, `face-atom/<group>` in the browser), never by handle index. It signs every act the device makes on the face's behalf (dial, join, enrolment, sibling channel, proof), the face's group secret seals to it, and it countersigns its own half of its delegation. It rides no bundle and no wire as a secret, and it ''never resurrects'': a lost device dies alone, and re-admission mints a fresh atom.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:58:''THE DYAD HAS ONE END'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). A dyad names one human's relationship with one device, and the device meets the face through its atom alone, so the dyad's end is the atom's DID. The word "veil" once named this per-device face; that face IS the atom now, and the face's fleet-wide presence is its persona root and its Handle. A vessel's own list of its atoms rests as a local custody row on no wire or board. Both ceremony sites MINT the dyad slot: a self-stood face signs its binding at the genesis epoch; a joinee lands `binding: null` (presented, not yet gathered), and the absence travels.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:165:A vessel carries ''one private plane per PersonaGroup'' — the multitude, the signer pin, the KEL prefix, the device edge. A human running several compartments on one laptop therefore needs several planes there, and the naming question follows immediately: ''how does a vessel find each plane without holding a thing that lists them?''
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:175:''The `@persona` stem names no bag.'' It carries the TITLE prefix every plane uses inside its own document — the signer pin, the KEL prefix, the selves, the bindings all spell `@persona/…` whichever plane holds them, because titles resolve verbatim within a document and the composite finds one by walking layers. It also carries the STEM a plane's derived id extends. Nothing answers to the bare stem.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle.mem:195:''`@` NAMES A BAG. Nothing else may wear it.'' A bag stands seeded, mounted, registered, cap-checked and reachable by its exact string. A ''board'' — the per-Nexus shared docs a Nexus keeps (KEL, members, antigen, who-face, crossroads) — stands at a deterministic doc url derived from a Nexus key, and ''that url CARRIES its whole identity''. Boards therefore read `board:<what-it-carries>` in every human-facing name, never `@something`. A diagnostic dressed as a bag id invites a reader to treat it as one, and the two kinds then sit a character apart in a grep — exactly how one gets mistaken for the other.
