ge-contents) · strike the CI compose job (B3) · one home for the migration headline, fixing two\nrestatements that say 111 where the certified figure is 110 (MIGRATION:36, SCOPE-NAMES:20) and noting\nLEDGER:1294's drifted 585/122/247 · weld the theme count and derive BAR from it (five instruments\nhardcode node_modules/tm-themes with no override, so the weld must read \"65 as this repository\nbundles them\") · fix four wrong present-tense counts (corpus-ledgers \"Thirteen\" vs 20, GateReport-Doc\n\"Seventeen\" vs 49/35/65, theme-model.test \"Four gates\" vs 12 requirers, tokenizer.test \"Twenty-two\"\nvs 26) without deleting the paragraphs · true still.js's 8 → 13 · MISS → dark/unmarked (one codec,\ntwo ledgers, one homonym).\nREVISED: share the two orphan sentinel STRINGS only, never the two baseline derivations (oracle\nreadAt vs a written control file — two of five containment grains) · generate the README's\ncompleteness CHECK, keep the curated prose, put only a --check arm in the manifest (25 distinct names,\n3 duplicated bullets, 37 of 49 gates unnamed, the weld asserting only existence) · move\ngate-report.js:18-25's archaeology to LEDGER:1809-1812, which already holds it — a move, never a\ndelete · snap-tid+snap-meta stays weakest-defended but do the collider fix first ·\ncorpus file naming to -ledger/-ratchet with direction in the first comment line · one lifecycle\nvocabulary (idle / unruled / retired), the winner already present in divergence-staleness:137.\nSTRUCK: swallow ⊂ still · dark-construct ∪ contrast-witness · the overreach glob fusion ·\nprose-reading-ledger ∪ PlainConstructs.\n\n## What YANG made visible to YIN\nThe invariants' structural exemption from the collider requirement · package-contents as a blocking\nuntested gate that C5 depends on · the 10 unused rulings being exactly the 10 READER 5.5.0-prerelease\nones, keyed out by the same module owedOf() would extend, 4 of them OWED · flags that switch\nPOPULATION rather than output, which withdraws all three \"one tool two gates\" candidates ·\nbacktrack-witness's wall-clock sensitivity, which any item changing what-runs-beside-what can move ·\nboth record welds reading the UNRELEASED section only, so new welds must key on something else.\n\n## Not verified this wave\nNo gate-report --check (writes two tracked files) · no .vsix extraction (listing only) · no\nstill --host --reach · no test-tools · the 63 punctuation.separator sites unread · the 423 OWED rows\nunread (330 of 359 in ablation-ledger, 93 of 111 in darkness-ledger) — deciding whether those two\nlargest corpus files are an enumerated backlog or a guard that stopped guarding · four of YANG's\nseams not taken up: overreach-cut's 0 exclusives, canary-memetic vs memetic-containment, the four\ncolour-reader CLI surfaces, the 6 invisible attic fixtures.\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0d19cbf6-cddf-4038-9616-34725eecb1d7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/VSCode-TW5-Syntax","sessionId":"7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9","version":"2.1.277","gitBranch":"release/3.0.0","slug":"luminous-strolling-torvalds"}
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/packages/lararium-sensorium/fixtures/claude-week-2026-10-07/sources/claude/7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9/subagents/agent-a876d2adbe9a4390a.jsonl:36:{"parentUuid":"23d2b639-d336-4966-91a0-a2a37abc053c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a876d2adbe9a4390a","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfoCYWWUQAfgZC9Ab4NDy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PQNGgLjVHWNM1XuEq9eCLk","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md","content":"# Consolidation plan — VSCode-TW5-Syntax harness and record\n\nWritten blind, from four survey files only. Every number below belongs to the pole that measured it\n(YIN or YANG, wave 1 or 2); where I write no attribution, the two poles agree. I have not read the\nrepository and I invent no figure. The plan is written for the hand that enacts it; where a step\ndepends on a measurement nobody took, the step says so and section F names what would settle it.\n\n## 0. The four goals, kept apart\n\n| Goal | What it means for this plan | Where it lands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Isomorphic by intent | one shape per QUESTION; look-alikes that answer different questions stay, and the record says on which axis | section A |\n| YIN collapse | fewer parts; every survivor earns its place by a named reader or a named question | section B, phases 1–2, 4–5 |\n| Aftermath / Evergreen | the record states what holds; a dated reading carries its date; a live claim has a machine twin | section B, phase 3 (the mark) |\n| L-Prime | prose names what a thing DOES; copulas that remain carry a confidence marker | section B, phase 6, scoped in section D |\n\nThe order of phases matters: collapse before the record pass (so the record describes the collapsed\ntree once), the mark before any figure is \"corrected\" (so a corrected figure cannot drift again), and\nL-Prime last (so prose is touched once, after the moves).\n\n## 0.1 Where the poles' numbers differ, and which difference matters\n\n| Figure | YIN | YANG | Why they differ | Matters to the plan? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fixtures whose vocabulary a snapshot already carries | 50 of 87 | 60 of 87 | extraction width (YANG: \"YIN measured by a narrower extraction\") | No. Both poles reject vocabulary subsumption as the criterion (C1); the ruling rests on YANG's 56-pin-a-law reading, not on 50 or 60. |\n| OWED rows, ablation / darkness | 330 of 359 / 93 of 111 | 319 / 92 | line grep vs reason-splitting (YANG) | No. The distinct-cause counts (22 and 5, YANG, every row read) are what the backlog ruling (C4) uses, and YANG states they are robust to the method. Use YANG's row counts for the ~400-line reduction estimate, since they are the reason-split ones. |\n| container stacks resolving identically | tool header: 44 of 65 | YANG re-run: 65 of 65 | a dated header integer vs a fresh reading | Only as an INSTANCE of the dated-reading defect (C5). The load-bearing claim (\"a container-grain gate would be worthless\") holds under either. |\n| `READER … OWED` rows | 76, all in darkness-ledger | 76: 65 darkness, 7 ablation, 4 expected-divergence | YANG's split is finer | Yes, in one way: all three files are read by stripping readers (YANG), so NO live fault exists today; the latent fault sits in swallow-ledger (5 READER rows, 0 OWED, read by light-cone without stripping). Step 1 targets that file. |\n| fixture population | 76 + 11 (+7 attic); \"87–89\" | 96 (76 + 11 + 6 attic + 2 known-gaps + 1 in samples) | population definition; attic 7 vs 6 unverified by either | Yes for scoping: the fixture ruling covers the 87 shared .tw5/.mem fixtures; attic, known-gaps and samples are protected separately (section E). |\n| still.js:35-38 class count | header says 8; YIN measured 13 (summing to 186 = still's own 186) | — | stale comment | Yes: it is the comment that caused YIN to rank a destructive collapse first in wave 1. Fix under the mark (phase 3). |\n| `source ./grammars.sh` inlined | twice | once (the second file reads grammars.sh as a file, a different act) | YANG read the act, not the string | Yes: follow YANG; one inlining to fix, not two. |\n| instruments | 65 non-test tools | 35 instruments behind 49 gates | different populations (all tools vs gate instruments) | Only for the \"Seventeen instruments\" fix: the replacement must say which population it counts, or derive it. |\n| theme-model readers | \"12 requirers\" | 12 non-test readers, 8 gates, 18 requirers total | YANG finer | Only for wording the replacement of \"Four gates\". |\n\n## A. The intent map\n\nEach group is one QUESTION. Inside a group, \"one shape\" means the members answer the question by\ndifferent means and should fuse; \"different grain\" names the axis on which they genuinely differ.\nSix axes come from the poles: population, scope, well-formedness, reader, grain, authority.\n\n### Q1. Does the grammar tokenize a given text as pinned?\n- 40 snapshots (737 distinct scopes, YIN) via snapshot-check.js — records what IS present; no syntax for absence (YANG).\n- 87 assertion fixtures (.tw5.test / .mem.test) — 1305 assertion lines over 415 distinct scopes (YIN); 36 carry negatives, 138 negatives total (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN from snapshots on well-formedness (absence) and on construct presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms appear in zero snapshots, YANG). Only 4 fixtures are behaviour-only (YANG): `blockquote`, `macro-string-params`, `mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines.\n- `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` — ONE SHAPE (one tool, one scope, two globs, populations 2 and 1; both poles concede).\n- `run_snapshots.sh`, `echo-test`, `list-test` — no reader (both). `tests-advanced/basic/htmltiddler/wikitext` — narrowings of one script (YIN), see F for the one check before striking them.\n- attic fixtures (6 YANG / 7 YIN), known-gaps (2), samples (1) — deliberately outside every glob, held by a README alone (YANG). Protected (E).\n- Follow YANG on grain: fixture ≠ snapshot. Follow both on snap-tid/snap-meta.\n\n### Q2. Where does the grammar disagree with the TW5 engine, and is each disagreement ruled?\n- `overreach` (31 exclusive rulings), `overreach-corpus-files` (57), `overreach-corpus-memetic` (2), `overreach-host` (2), `overreach-cut` (0); union 101 of 111; 10 unused = exactly the 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings keyed out by reader-scope.js (all YANG).\n- `expected-divergence` (the rulings), `divergence-staleness` (derives its run set FROM package.json — YIN A4 cites :48-51; YANG agrees), `reader-scope.js` (`readerOf`), `degraded-ground.test.js`.\n- DIFFERENT GRAIN on population: each arm is a different ground, and overreach-check.js:306 takes one pattern only (YIN A4) — the glob fusion is STRUCK by YIN itself. overreach-cut is the sole member with nothing exclusive; see C3.\n- Follow YANG's coupling measurement entirely; it is the strongest number in either report.\n\n### Q3. Does the engine compile the grammar, and does it behave as the model predicts?\n- `engine-witness` vs `engine-behaviour` — compile vs behaviour; engine-ledger's one ruling is BEHAVIOUR-prefixed and filtered out of the compile arm (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (well-formedness vs behaviour). Keep.\n- `theme-model` vs `theme-collision` — model vs the only reader of what vscode-textmate paints (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep.\n\n### Q4. How much of the corpus does the grammar read fully, and what remains unasked?\n- `still` (sampled, `--sample 25`, draw-dependent: seeds 1/2/9/13 green, seed 5 red on one tree, YANG) vs `still --reach` (whole; carrier floor seated at 0.9947; 23 of 4,403 carriers hold an unnamed class, so a draw of 25 misses all 23 seven times in eight, YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (population). Keep both.\n- `swallow-witness` — same 186 instances as still over the corpus (YIN A5), but alone produces `unasked-regions-ceiling` (97 regions, 83 open under some cut, 14 unasked, YIN; seated at 14, YANG) and an 18-region probe-escape reading. DIFFERENT QUESTION (coverage share vs region ceiling). Fusion STRUCK by YIN, defended by YANG — closed.\n- `carrier-reach-floor`, `coverage-floor`, `ruling-breadth-ceiling`, `still-breadth.js` (runs as a child process in sandboxes, YIN) — ratchets and helpers of this question. YANG names \"six coverage authorities\" without listing them; I cannot map the sixth and do not try.\n- `carrier-ledger` (7 rows) vs `swallow-ledger` — identical row grammar; carrier-ledger refuses fusion in its own header; `still --over ./corpus --reach` reads 52 of 52 carriers, 100.0%, floor 99.5%, without consulting the corpus ledger (YIN A3). Consistent with the header; not proven — wants the host sweep (F).\n\n### Q5. Does a construct take only its own scopes (containment)?\n- Five grains (YANG): `bleed-canary` / `composition-check` / `swallow-witness` / `memetic-containment` / `corpus-check`. Plus `canary-tid` (2 files) and `canary-memetic` (4) — what canary-memetic catches that memetic-containment does not: UNMEASURED (YANG, YIN did not take it up).\n- bleed-canary vs corpus-check containment: three sentinel STRINGS, two baseline derivations (oracle `readAt().kind === 'built'` vs a written control file) (YIN D2). ONE SHAPE for the strings; DIFFERENT GRAIN for the baselines (two of the five). Both poles now agree: share strings only.\n\n### Q6. Can anything reach a given scope?\n- `scope-can-appear` vs `grammar-reachability` vs `corpus-check` coverage — three causes of \"nothing reaches it\" (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep; no pole contested.\n\n### Q7. Is the grammar legible under the bundled themes?\n- `dark-construct` (absent rule) vs `contrast-witness` (rule within CIE76 ΔE of the default) vs `theme-parity` (below the median of six comparator grammars) — three MECHANISMS (YANG); 22 vs 26 findings, union 47, intersection 1, structurally exclusive per theme (YIN A1). DIFFERENT GRAIN. Keep all three; the duplicated anecdote (one event, three headers) gets ONE home. Both agree.\n- `colour-witness`, `colour-pair` (exists as both a ledger and a floor, YIN V1), `legibility-floor`, `prose-reading-ledger` (1 row) vs `PlainConstructs.tid` (20 keys) — intersection 0, split by FAULT is right, split by STORAGE is arbitrary (YIN A2). Keep both populations; the storage split is a Q-record question, not a collapse.\n- `theme-paint` / `family-atlas` / `page-palette` / `reading-recipe` — CLI surfaces may merge, questions may not; page-palette's question is read by nothing else (YANG). HELD (F).\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — the only implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). PROTECTED (E).\n- Theme population: node_modules/tm-themes hardcoded in five instruments, no override; 65 (both); `BAR = 16` literal vs \"a quarter of 65\" in prose (YIN R4).\n\n### Q8. Which rulings are owed, which hold, and what retires?\n- Six `/^OWED/` readings: ablation-witness:361, darkness-witness:219, swallow-witness:107-108 strip `READER <v>` via the shared reader; light-cone:81, bracket-witness:155, delimiter-inheritance.test:96,106 do not (YANG A3; YIN counted five sites, YANG six). ONE SHAPE: one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`.\n- Ledgers (12 ledger-shaped files, 10 named `-ledger`, YIN V1): ablation, darkness, swallow, bracket, carrier, engine, colour-pair, prose-reading, expected-divergence, must-fail, and the ones unnamed in the reports. Seven single-number ratchets, 4 floor / 3 ceiling; `attribute-kind-ceiling` holds a count that may FALL (direction misnamed once, YIN V1).\n- Lifecycle vocabulary: stale / explains nothing / retires / unruled / idle — one lifecycle, winner `idle` already at divergence-staleness:137 (YIN).\n- `MISS` is a homonym across two ledgers sharing one codec (YIN V3).\n- The two largest ledgers: ablation 359 rows / darkness 111 rows, 22 and 5 distinct causes (YANG) — see C4.\n\n### Q9. Does the harness guard itself?\n- `every-gate-collides` — MEASURED FALSE GREEN: name matched over raw text, clause over comment-stripped code (YANG; YIN B1 confirms lines 67/77 and calls it a GRAIN fault). Four CI gates outside it (`edition:check`, `lint-closure`, `package-contents`, `tests-known-gaps`); `package-contents.js` has no test and no collider (YANG). No invariant ever enters the pairing — exemption by construction, recorded nowhere (YIN B4: 21 of 39 carry a collider, 18 do not; YANG: 25 of 39 show no marker — different markers counted, neither matters once the exemption is written down).\n- `one-implementation` — fingerprint scan excludes `*.test.js` (POPULATION fault per YIN; a reasoned exclusion with a five-entry named exemption map per YANG). See C2.\n- `every-test-runs` — collects `*.test.js` only; all 96 fixtures outside it (YANG).\n- `gate-report --check` — NOT read-only: `ceiling` (textmate-ceiling.js --write) and `builtins` (builtins-sync.js without --check) write tracked files; both have --check arms the manifest does not use (YANG; YIN B2 names the law and two correct precedents: edition:build/check, signals/signals:check).\n- gate-report's SKIP regex vs a reasons list — two differently-keyed \"what is not a gate\" enumerations; the regex once hid two CI instruments (YIN R8). HELD (F).\n- CI: `compose` job is the only true duplicate (same ubuntu/node22 platform as `gates`, YIN B3); the grammar job's repeats are a four-way OS/node matrix; test.yml:88-95 re-running four gates is what makes them blocking (YANG) — PROTECTED (E). Dirty-tree guard (test.yml:60-69) runs in `grammar`, not `gates`.\n\n### Q10. Does the record match the tree?\n- Migration headline in four places; three hand-written restatement regexes; MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 say 111 where the certified figure is 110; LEDGER:1294 says 585/122/247 against 556/110/245 (YIN R1, R2; YANG concedes and adds \"one file contradicts itself 31 lines apart\").\n- \"65 bundled themes\" in 8 files, welded nowhere (YIN R3). 106 N/65-or-percent figures in the LEDGER, two welds, both reading the UNRELEASED section only (YANG). 6 `# checks:` machine twins in the whole repository, across 3 of 20 corpus files, none in the LEDGER (YIN).\n- README gate list: 25 distinct names, 3 duplicated bullets, 37 of 49 gates unnamed; the weld checks only that a named script exists (YIN R6).\n- Four wrong present-tense counts (both poles, all four confirmed by YANG): corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20); GateReport-Doc.tid \"Seventeen instruments\" (49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools); theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates, 12 non-test readers, 18 requirers); tokenizer.test.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test, 35 total).\n- Three colour-cluster header integers: reproduce in rank, direction and ratio, not in integer (YANG A2).\n- ONE SHAPE across all of these: a figure is either dated or derived. This is the mark (B, phase 3).\n\n### Q11. What ships?\n- `.vscodeignore`, `package-contents.js` (manifest → package only, never package → needed; YIN I, YANG 4). `vsce ls` shows 34 files: syntaxes/attic (14,013 + 936 bytes), docs/prior-art (seven documents, 280,996 bytes), nine shipped .mem documents + attic = 349,604 bytes (YIN A6, listing only, no extraction). `.vscodeignore:16` references run_snapshots.sh (YANG).\n\n### Q12. Does the grammar stall?\n- `backtrack-witness` — wall-clock: 1.4 ms alone vs past 8 ms under the suite, 0 vs 6 stalls between two runs at rest (YANG). Keep; every phase that changes what runs beside it must re-measure (F).\n\n## B. The ordered plan\n\nEach step: WHAT changes · WHAT is lost · MUST EXIST FIRST · EVIDENCE. No step depends on a later one.\n\"Collider\" means the `--must-fail` arm every gate owes under every-gate-collides.\n\n### Phase 0 — make the verifier honest (nothing is collapsed yet)\n\n**0.1 every-gate-collides strips comments before the NAME match** (the clause match already does).\nLost: nothing; at least one currently-green gate will red (YIN B1 cost asymmetry) — that red is the\nfinding, fix the collider it exposes, do not revert. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 1 (deleting\ntheme-collision.test.js's real `--must-fail` arm keeps the invariant green); corpus-ledgers.test.js:43\nis the worked example (YIN B1).\nWhy before step 1: every later step that moves or deletes a test file is certified by this invariant;\nstep 1 itself must carry delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107's collider across (YIN B3). A blind\ncollider check cannot certify that.\n\n**0.2 the manifest names only --check arms**: `ceiling` → textmate-ceiling.js --check,\n`builtins` → builtins-sync.js --check. Lost: nothing; the write arms stay as scripts for the hand.\nFirst: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 2; YIN B2 law (\"a harvest's CHECK arm belongs in the manifest,\nits WRITE arm never does\") and its two existing precedents.\nWhy here: the enacting hand runs `gate-report --check` after every phase; today that dirties the tree\ntwice per run and the dirty-tree guard sits in the other CI job.\n\n**0.3 write the exemption down**: one line in the LEDGER (or wherever rulings live) stating that\ninvariants enter no collider pairing by construction, and that the four CI gates outside the\ninvariant are listed by name. Lost: nothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 4–5, YIN B4.\nThis is a record act, but it must precede any step that would otherwise look like it removed a\ncollider obligation.\n\n### Phase 1 — the one latent fault that disarms a gate\n\n**1.1 one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()` in reader-scope.js; all six sites call it.**\nLost: nothing today — all 76 `READER … OWED` rows live in files whose readers already strip (YANG\nA3: 65/7/4). First: 0.1 (to certify the collider carried across). Evidence: YANG A3 — light-cone and\nswallow-witness read the SAME file with incompatible predicates; swallow-ledger holds five READER rows\nand zero OWED, and adding OWED to any one of them makes light-cone's one-sided gate read it as RULED\nand go silent. YIN D4 first named it; YANG promoted it to first.\nCollider: a `READER 5.4.1 OWED` row in a sandboxed swallow-ledger must red light-cone after the fix\nand must be shown green-by-silence before it (the RED-TESTS-FIRST control).\n\n**Do I agree with \"first\"?** Yes on substance: it precedes every collapse, because every rename of\na ledger (phase 4) and every re-keying of the backlog ledgers (C4) passes through this reader, and a\nre-key through six divergent predicates would be six faults. No on the literal position: phase 0 is\nwhat makes step 1.1's own verification trustworthy, and phase 0 costs three lines. If the hand must\npick a single step and stop, pick 1.1.\n\n### Phase 2 — pure removals (no record edit, no rename)\n\n**2.1 delete `run_snapshots.sh` + `echo-test` + `list-test` AND `.vscodeignore:16` together.**\nLost: nothing (zero readers, both poles). First: nothing. Evidence: YIN dead list; YANG's coupling\n(the package gate never reports a stale ignore entry).\n\n**2.2 strike the four narrowing test aliases** (82 → 75 scripts, YIN wave 2 — the 82 → 75 arithmetic\nis YIN's; 82 − 3 − 4 = 75 checks against 2.1). Lost: four names. First: the one check in F.1 (that the\numbrella script runs every population an alias ran, since every-test-runs does not see fixtures).\nEvidence: YIN; YANG concedes.\n\n**2.3 strike the CI `compose` job.** Lost: nothing (same platform as `gates`, YIN B3). First: nothing.\nMust NOT touch: test.yml:88-95 and the grammar job's matrix (E). Evidence: YIN B3; YANG concedes item 6.\n\n**2.4 fuse `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` into one script with two globs.** Lost: one line in the harvest\nsummary — YANG: the per-arm line is habit, no file states 49. First: 0.1; and read F.3 before\nenacting (a grep of the record for the literal gate count). Evidence: both concede; populations 2 and\n1; a different invariant holds the file associations (YANG).\n\n**2.5 the two real duplications the ward cannot see**: the byte-identical `scopesOn` in exactly two\ninvariants → tokenizer.js's helper; the one inlined `source ./grammars.sh` → the shared act; rename\nthe THIRD same-named different function (offset-based, returns an array — YANG A4). Lost: nothing.\nFirst: nothing — \"both fixable today without touching the ward\" (YANG). Evidence: YIN D8 counts\n(two helpers right), YANG's correction (one inlining, not two).\n\n**2.6 share the three sentinel STRINGS in sentinel.js; keep both baseline derivations.** Lost:\nnothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YIN D2 revised in wave 2; YANG \"strings only\".\n\n### Phase 3 — the mark, then every figure under it\n\n**3.1 define ONE syntax for a figure**: either `<figure> (read <date>, over <population>)` or a\nmachine-readable twin (the `# checks:` form already present in 6 places — reuse it, do not invent a\nsecond). Add ONE gate: a figure in a welded file carrying neither fails. Key the gate on the mark, NOT\non the UNRELEASED section (both existing welds go blind the day 3.1.0 opens — YANG).\nLost: nothing. First: nothing structural; but it comes AFTER phases 1–2 so the first derived figures\ndescribe the collapsed tree, and BEFORE 3.2–3.6 so each fix is an instance of the mark rather than a\none-time correction. Evidence: YANG's blind-spot section (\"arrived at from four directions\"); YIN R5\n(6 twins in the whole repository, none in 1840 LEDGER lines).\nWhere it sits in my order: third phase, gating all record work. YANG wanted it second; the only\ndifference is that I let the pure removals (phase 2) go first because they change counts the mark\nwould otherwise date twice.\n\n**3.2 one home for the migration headline** (556/110/245); the other three sites either derive it or\ncarry a date; fix MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 (111 → 110); mark LEDGER:1294's 585/122/247 as the\ndated sequence it narrates, do not \"correct\" it. The three restatement regexes become ONE weld keyed\non the mark. Evidence: YIN R1–R2; YANG concedes \"a correctness fix\".\nCaution (F.2): neither pole says whether the \"110\" certified migration figure and the \"111 rulings in\nexpected-divergence\" (YANG) count the same thing. Resolve before editing, or the fix may be wrong.\n\n**3.3 weld the theme count and derive BAR from it.** The weld reads \"65 as this repository bundles\nthem\" (five instruments hardcode node_modules/tm-themes, no override — YIN wave 2 wording); contrast-\nwitness's `BAR = 16` becomes a derivation from that count. Evidence: YIN R3–R4; YANG \"concede and\nWIDEN\".\n\n**3.4 fix the four wrong present-tense counts WITHOUT deleting the paragraphs** — each becomes either\nderived or dated: corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20), GateReport-Doc \"Seventeen instruments\"\n(say which population: 49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools, or defer to the manifest as the tool's own\nheader already does), theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates of 12 non-test readers), tokenizer.\ntest.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test of 35). Two of these headers are load-bearing prose (E); the number\nchanges, the law stays. Evidence: both poles, all four confirmed by YANG.\n\n**3.5 true still.js:35-38 (8 → 13 classes)**, dated. Evidence: YIN A5.\n\n**3.6 the three colour-cluster header integers get the date and population they were read over**\n(44 of 65; 1,191 of 11,756 = 10.1%; 18 of 54 = 33%). Do NOT overwrite with YANG's re-run (65 of 65;\n1,467 of 15,116 = 9.7%; 23 of 59 = 39%) — that would be a second undated reading; see C5. The shared\nanecdote (one event in three headers) moves to ONE home in the LEDGER, each header keeping one line\nthat names its MECHANISM. Evidence: YANG A2; YIN A1 (two lines of duplicated anecdote is all that\nsurvives of D3).\n\n**3.7 generate the README gate list's COMPLETENESS check; keep the curated prose; manifest gets only\nthe --check arm.** Evidence: YIN R6 revised; YANG concedes 9.\n\n**3.8 move gate-report.js:18-25 (the removed-flag archaeology) to LEDGER:1809-1812 which already\nholds it — a move, never a delete — and carry the 38-46 reader-keyed routing note's LAW with it in\nthe LEDGER while LEAVING the comment in place (no test holds it; YANG). Evidence: YIN 16 revised;\nYANG \"move, never lose\".\n\n### Phase 4 — vocabulary (one word per relation)\n\nEach rename passes through `owedOf`/`readerOf` and the ledger-shape module; hence after phase 1.\n\n**4.1 corpus file naming → `-ledger` / `-ratchet`, direction in the first comment line** (fixes the\none filename whose direction is wrong: `attribute-kind-ceiling` may FALL — YIN V1; confirm the\ncomparator before renaming, F.6). Collider obligations move with the names (YIN B4). Evidence: YIN\nV1; YANG concedes 13.\n**4.2 `MISS` → `dark` / `unmarked`** (one codec, two ledgers, one homonym). Evidence: YIN V3; YANG 14.\n**4.3 one lifecycle word**: `idle` (already at divergence-staleness:137) / `unruled` / `retired`.\nEvidence: YIN V4; YANG 15.\n**4.4 one spelling of over-reading** (OVERREACH / overbound / overreach — YIN V2). Nobody ranked it;\nit is a grep-and-replace once 4.1 settles the ledger names, with the weld test first\n(signing-domain grep per the house rule).\n\n### Phase 5 — conditional items (each has a named condition; do not enact without it)\n\n**5.1 syntaxes/attic (and docs/prior-art) out of the .vsix.** Condition: build and EXTRACT a .vsix\nfirst (YIN listed only); land WITH the first collider package-contents.js has ever had, and extend\npackage-contents to check package → needed, not only manifest → package. The attic STAYS IN THE TREE\nwith its README (E). Evidence: YIN A6; YANG 17 + fault 4.\n**5.2 retire `overreach-cut`.** Condition: degraded-ground.test.js resolves its TiddlyWiki host so it\nRUNS in CI (today it skips unless a checkout sits as a sibling — YANG B). Then the gate has 0 exclusive\nrulings and a byte-identical reader. See C3.\n**5.3 the backlog collapse (ablation 319 → 22 causes, darkness 92 → 5).** Condition, YANG's,\nnon-negotiable: the row grammar carries a `cause` identifier distinct from `key`; the breadth ratchet\ncounts distinct causes; a single-number ratchet seated at 1 refuses a ruling absorbing more than one\ncause, header stating why (the predecessor re-seated four times in one session: 55.8 → 56.6 → 57.2 →\n60.0%). See C4. After phase 1 and 4.1, since it re-keys rows every OWED reader parses.\n**5.4 fuse the four behaviour-only fixtures into snapshots** (`blockquote`, `macro-string-params`,\n`mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines, YANG). Condition: a snapshot sample exists that carries\neach construct (YANG confirmed for macro-string-params: 14 named-argument calls). Fix `blockquote`'s\nwrong header (\"Test for header syntaxes\") whether or not it fuses. See C1.\n**5.5 one home for judgements (corpus/*.txt vs JSON data tiddlers — YIN D5).** Condition: a stated\nreason per surface why it is text or tiddler. YIN A2 already found the storage split arbitrary for one\npair; nobody measured the other eight tiddlers. Record the axis; do not move data until it is read.\n**5.6 the four colour-reader CLI surfaces.** HELD. Condition: a reading of what page-palette answers\nthat nothing else does (YANG). No measurement exists; no step.\n**5.7 canary-memetic vs memetic-containment.** HELD; unmeasured by either pole.\n\n### Phase 6 — the Evergreen / L-Prime pass (section D), last, once.\n\n## C. The adjudications\n\n**C1. Fixture collapse (50 vs 60; vocabulary subsumption).**\nRuling: vocabulary subsumption is NOT a safe criterion, and the 50/60 gap is irrelevant to that.\nI follow YANG, on two measured properties: 36 of 87 fixtures carry 138 negative assertions and the\nsnapshot format has no syntax for absence (29 of the 60 would be lost outright); and construct\npresence ≠ vocabulary presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms in zero snapshots, three in\nzero broad-corpus files, one under a live OWED ruling). YIN itself flagged in wave 1 that its measure\nwas vocabulary not scope-at-position and handed the law question to YANG. Result: 4 fusible (5.4),\n83 stay. What would change it: a snapshot format that records absence AND a scope-at-position\nmeasurement showing the remaining fixtures add no position a snapshot lacks. Either alone is not enough.\n\n**C2. one-implementation's `*.test.js` exclusion.**\nRuling: keep the exclusion; extend the existing five-entry named-exemption map to the fingerprint scan\n(YANG A4). YIN's \"population fault\" reading was right that the ward cannot see two real duplications;\nYANG's reading of the reason two lines above the exclusion is decisive: a test must hold its gate's\ncollider, and the no-second-implementation rule applied to tests would forbid the collision the house\nrequires. Lifting the exclusion WIDENS obligations and needs an allowance list (YIN B1 concedes the\ncost). The two duplications are fixed in 2.5 without the ward. What would change it: if the exemption\nmap cannot express \"a test may carry its collided instrument's body\" — then write that rule as a new\nmap entry kind, still not a blanket lift.\n\n**C3. overreach-cut's CI reachability.**\nRuling: YANG's condition, in YANG's order. On the operator's machine degraded-ground.test.js runs the\ncut arm with a byte-identical invocation; in CI it skips, so the gate is the only reader there and a\nconditional skip reads as a pass (YANG fragile set). Fix the test's host resolution, confirm it runs in\nCI (a green that is a run, not a skip), then drop the script entry — and because divergence-staleness\nderives its run set from package.json, confirm the arm's exclusive count is still 0 at that moment\n(it was 0 over the current ruling file; it is not a property of the arm). What would change it: a\nnon-zero exclusive after the host fix, or no way to give CI a host — then the gate stays and its\nheader says why.\n\n**C4. The debt backlogs and the one-cause ratchet.**\nRuling: YANG's concession with YANG's condition. The two files hold 22 and 5 causes enumerated at\ncharacter-position grain because the key grammar forces one row per position (YANG, every reason\nread; darkness's largest cause, 89 rows, is stated again in the file's own header). They are an\nenumerated backlog, not 319 and 92 guards. The 330/93 vs 319/92 difference is method and does not\ntouch the ruling. The condition is non-negotiable because the only evidence we have of what happens\nwithout it is a ratchet re-seated four times in one session with every reason true. Order: after\nphase 1 (owedOf) and 4.1 (names), since it rewrites rows six readers parse. What would change it:\nevidence that some rows within one \"cause\" family are read individually by a gate (a row-grain reader\nnobody named) — then the family cannot collapse to one row.\n\n**C5. The colour cluster's irreproducible header integers.**\nRuling: they are dated readings in the present tense — YANG's own finding, \"one defect, not two\".\nRank, direction and ratio reproduce (YANG: variable first both times; quiet share 9.7% vs 10.1%);\nthe integers do not, and the population moved (59 vs 54 themes ruling `variable`; 15,116 vs 11,756\nrule-parts). Do not rewrite 44 → 65; date the 44 and name its population, or replace the integer with\na derivation that produces today's number as a machine twin. Three gates stay (three mechanisms, and\nYIN's union/intersection measurement makes fusion lose 46 of 47 findings). What would change it: a\nderivation script shipped with the module — then the live number replaces the dated one and the\nheader carries no integer at all.\n\n**Closed by the poles themselves, recorded here so no hand reopens them:** swallow ⊂ still (YIN\nstruck; stale \"8 classes\" was the cause); dark-construct ∪ contrast-witness (YIN struck: intersection\n1 of 47); the overreach glob fusion (YIN struck on overreach-check.js:306 and divergence-staleness's\nmanifest coupling); prose-reading-ledger ∪ PlainConstructs (YIN struck: intersection 0); the three\nflag-pairs as \"one tool two gates\" (withdrawn — flags switch POPULATION, not output, per YANG; YIN\naccepts).\n\n## D. The Evergreen / L-Prime pass, as work\n\nNumbers here are YIN's; YANG did not verify copula densities.\n\n**What the pass touches, in order of yield:**\n1. Harness comment archaeology (the record is MORE evergreen than the comments — YIN): gate-report.js:18-25\n   (moved in 3.8); coverage-floor's header (13 of 24 lines narrate two re-seats → one line stating the\n   current seat with its date, the two re-seats to the LEDGER); ruling-breadth-ceiling and must-fail\n   headers (same shape; YIN names them, gives no counts).\n2. The LEDGER: 127 copulas at 0.07/line, dominated by explanatory tails (\"which is\", \"is the\", \"is\n   what\"). Turn the tails; where a copula states a ruling, leave it and mark confidence. The 106\n   N-of-65 figures are handled by the mark (3.1), not by prose.\n3. README, MIGRATION, CHANGELOG at 0.04–0.06/line. The CHANGELOG is dated by construction — past\n   entries are readings with a date already; Evergreen touches only its UNRELEASED section. MIGRATION's\n   sentences that a weld regex reads change ONLY together with the weld (measure both sides).\n4. The four wrong-count paragraphs (3.4) — the number is fixed under the mark; the sentence around it\n   is turned to say what the count GUARDS (\"N instruments require this module, so a change here reaches\n   N gates\"), which is the L-Prime shape anyway.\n\n**What the pass must not touch:**\n- docs/scope-naming-prior-art.mem: 84 of its 87 copulas sit inside a VERBATIM handback (YIN). An\n  L-Prime pass there destroys the \"verbatim\" claim. Only the 3 outside the quotation are in scope.\n- contributing.md: zero copulas (YIN). Nothing to do; do not \"improve\" it.\n- The 19 load-bearing prose sites (section E): rephrase in place is allowed; moving, shortening or\n  deleting is not, unless the law is first carried into a test or a LEDGER row and the comment points at it.\n- Any sentence a weld regex reads (the three migration restatements, the README gate-list weld) —\n  change only with its weld.\n- Quoted error strings, operator-spoken rulings quoted as such, and fixture header lines that state a\n  law (YANG: 4 fixtures pin their law in the header line).\n\n**How a hand tells the difference, three questions per sentence:**\n1. Is it inside a fence, a quotation, or a block marked verbatim/handback? → leave it.\n2. Does an instrument read it (a weld regex, a `# checks:` twin, a header line a test greps)? → change\n   it only with that reader, and run the reader red first.\n3. Does it state a law nothing else holds (the E list, or any \"do not\" / \"refused\" / \"deferred\" with\n   no test beside it)? → keep the law's content word-for-word, turn only the copula, and never move it alone.\nIf all three answer no, turn it freely: name what the thing does, date any number, derive any count.\n\n## E. What I refuse to let a tidy-up touch\n\nEach entry: the thing · why it looks removable · the line to read first.\n- LEDGER:856-861 — the within-construct distinctness witness is DEFERRED BY RULING; looks like a gap. Read the row.\n- LEDGER:851-855 — the fragment left unarticulated on purpose; looks like an unfinished note.\n- LEDGER:780-790 — underline as one stroke; the ruling the uri-span scouts evidence.\n- LEDGER:836-850 — two REFUSED palette moves; look like TODOs.\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — look like dead scouts; the ONLY implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). No ward reads them; nothing else does either.\n- `still-breadth.js`, `tw5-oracle-path.js` — look unreferenced; run as child processes inside swapped-grammar sandboxes (YIN).\n- The shared modules (sentinel, region-kind, carrier-reading, ledger-shape, walk, jsonc, tokenizer, run-tool) — each header records the drift it prevents; they are the RESULTS of earlier collapses. one-implementation catches a re-expansion, not a wrong fusion (YANG).\n- tests/memetic-wikitext/attic/README.md and the 6-or-7 attic fixtures; syntaxes/attic/README.md — held by a README alone, invisible to every glob BY DESIGN (YANG). The attic may leave the PACKAGE (5.1), never the tree.\n- CI test.yml:88-95 — re-running four gates in the blocking job is what makes them blocking. A duplication reading un-blocks four gates silently (YANG). test.yml:60-69 — the dirty-tree guard's reason.\n- gate-report.js:38-46 — reader-keyed report routing, held by no test; and its SKIP regex (hid two CI instruments once — change only with the reasons list, F.5).\n- The 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings — \"unused\" only because reader-scope keys them out; 4 are OWED. Not stale; do not retire.\n- `overreach-corpus-memetic`'s 2 exclusive rulings — fusing that arm retires the ruling that makes the dialect readable (YANG).\n- engine-ledger's single BEHAVIOUR-prefixed ruling — filtered out of the compile arm on purpose.\n- carrier-ledger's header — refuses the swallow fusion in its own words; YIN's measurement is consistent with it.\n- `macro-dynamic-params` — 76 assertions, six of seven forms in zero snapshots, one under a live OWED ruling.\n- backtrack-witness.test.js:48-66; a-lar-uri-reads-without-underline's header; corpus-ledgers.test.js header (\"Thirteen\" — fix the number, keep the law); coverage-floor, ruling-breadth-ceiling, carrier-reach-floor, legibility-floor, colour-pair-ledger headers; delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107 (the collider owedOf must carry).\n- Conditional skips (checkout / seed / plugin absent) — they read as passes; do not convert a skip into a deletion of the gate it stands for.\n- The two baseline derivations in containment (oracle readAt vs written control file) — two of five grains, not two sentinels.\n- The three colour mechanisms' header lines naming the mechanism (one anecdote moves; three mechanisms stay).\n\n## F. What neither pole measured, and what would settle it\n\n1. **What runs the 96 fixtures** (every-test-runs sees none of them — YANG). Before 2.2 strikes the four\n   aliases: diff the fixture population each alias runs against the umbrella script's. Settles whether\n   an alias is a narrowing or the only runner of a family.\n2. **110 vs 111.** YIN's certified migration figure is 110; YANG counts 111 rulings in expected-divergence.\n   Same thing or two things? One grep of what the weld certifies. Settles whether 3.2 is a fix or a mistake.\n3. **Whether any file states the gate count** (\"49 of 49\" — YIN D; \"no file states 49\" — YANG). A grep for\n   the literal in the record. Settles the cost of 2.4 and 5.2.\n4. **`still --host --reach` over ~4,403 carriers** — settles carrier-ledger's 7 rows (YIN A3 is corpus-only).\n5. **The SKIP regex vs the reasons list** (YIN R8): which names the four CI gates outside the collider?\n   Settles whether 0.3 can key on one enumeration.\n6. **`attribute-kind-ceiling`'s comparator direction** — confirm the count may fall before 4.1 renames it.\n7. **The 63 `punctuation.separator` sites** — a fifth naming law or accretion (YIN V6/J). Settles whether\n   SCOPE-NAMES gains a rule or the sites get re-scoped; out of this plan's scope until read.\n8. **backtrack-witness under the collapsed suite** — re-measure after phases 2 and 5; the witness is\n   wall-clock and any change in what-runs-beside-what can move it (YANG).\n9. **The `# checks:` twin mechanism** — what reads the 6 twins today. Settles whether the mark (3.1) reuses\n   it or needs a second reader (it must not need a second).\n10. **A .vsix extracted, not listed** — settles 5.1's byte figures and whether docs/prior-art is wanted in\n    the package.\n11. **The four CLI colour surfaces and canary-memetic** — unmeasured by both; held until a reading exists.\n12. **Copula densities** — YIN's only; YANG did not verify. A second count before phase 6 is cheap.\n\n## G. The short form, for the hand\n\n0. Collider strips comments before the name match · manifest names only --check arms · write the invariants' exemption down.\n1. `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`, six sites, collider carried, red-first on a sandboxed swallow-ledger.\n2. Delete three dead scripts + `.vscodeignore:16` · strike four aliases (after F.1) · strike CI compose · fuse snap-tid/snap-meta · fix two duplications + one misnamed function · share three sentinel strings.\n3. Define the dated-or-derived mark with one gate keyed on the mark · then headline/110 (after F.2), theme count + BAR, four counts, 8→13, three colour integers dated, README check generated, gate-report archaeology moved.\n4. `-ledger`/`-ratchet` names with direction · MISS → dark/unmarked · idle/unruled/retired · one spelling of overreach.\n5. Conditionals, each behind its condition: attic out of the .vsix with package-contents' first collider · overreach-cut after degraded-ground runs in CI · backlogs after the cause field and the one-cause ratchet · four fixtures after their snapshot samples exist.\n6. L-Prime/Evergreen, once, with the three questions in D, never inside the verbatim handback.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7061,"cache_read_input_tokens":57622,"output_tokens":16081,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7061},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":16081,"cache_read_input_tokens":57622,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7061,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7061,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PQNGgLjVHWNM1XuEq9eCLk":{"file_path":"/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md","content":"# Consolidation plan — VSCode-TW5-Syntax harness and record\n\nWritten blind, from four survey files only. Every number below belongs to the pole that measured it\n(YIN or YANG, wave 1 or 2); where I write no attribution, the two poles agree. I have not read the\nrepository and I invent no figure. The plan is written for the hand that enacts it; where a step\ndepends on a measurement nobody took, the step says so and section F names what would settle it.\n\n## 0. The four goals, kept apart\n\n| Goal | What it means for this plan | Where it lands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Isomorphic by intent | one shape per QUESTION; look-alikes that answer different questions stay, and the record says on which axis | section A |\n| YIN collapse | fewer parts; every survivor earns its place by a named reader or a named question | section B, phases 1–2, 4–5 |\n| Aftermath / Evergreen | the record states what holds; a dated reading carries its date; a live claim has a machine twin | section B, phase 3 (the mark) |\n| L-Prime | prose names what a thing DOES; copulas that remain carry a confidence marker | section B, phase 6, scoped in section D |\n\nThe order of phases matters: collapse before the record pass (so the record describes the collapsed\ntree once), the mark before any figure is \"corrected\" (so a corrected figure cannot drift again), and\nL-Prime last (so prose is touched once, after the moves).\n\n## 0.1 Where the poles' numbers differ, and which difference matters\n\n| Figure | YIN | YANG | Why they differ | Matters to the plan? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fixtures whose vocabulary a snapshot already carries | 50 of 87 | 60 of 87 | extraction width (YANG: \"YIN measured by a narrower extraction\") | No. Both poles reject vocabulary subsumption as the criterion (C1); the ruling rests on YANG's 56-pin-a-law reading, not on 50 or 60. |\n| OWED rows, ablation / darkness | 330 of 359 / 93 of 111 | 319 / 92 | line grep vs reason-splitting (YANG) | No. The distinct-cause counts (22 and 5, YANG, every row read) are what the backlog ruling (C4) uses, and YANG states they are robust to the method. Use YANG's row counts for the ~400-line reduction estimate, since they are the reason-split ones. |\n| container stacks resolving identically | tool header: 44 of 65 | YANG re-run: 65 of 65 | a dated header integer vs a fresh reading | Only as an INSTANCE of the dated-reading defect (C5). The load-bearing claim (\"a container-grain gate would be worthless\") holds under either. |\n| `READER … OWED` rows | 76, all in darkness-ledger | 76: 65 darkness, 7 ablation, 4 expected-divergence | YANG's split is finer | Yes, in one way: all three files are read by stripping readers (YANG), so NO live fault exists today; the latent fault sits in swallow-ledger (5 READER rows, 0 OWED, read by light-cone without stripping). Step 1 targets that file. |\n| fixture population | 76 + 11 (+7 attic); \"87–89\" | 96 (76 + 11 + 6 attic + 2 known-gaps + 1 in samples) | population definition; attic 7 vs 6 unverified by either | Yes for scoping: the fixture ruling covers the 87 shared .tw5/.mem fixtures; attic, known-gaps and samples are protected separately (section E). |\n| still.js:35-38 class count | header says 8; YIN measured 13 (summing to 186 = still's own 186) | — | stale comment | Yes: it is the comment that caused YIN to rank a destructive collapse first in wave 1. Fix under the mark (phase 3). |\n| `source ./grammars.sh` inlined | twice | once (the second file reads grammars.sh as a file, a different act) | YANG read the act, not the string | Yes: follow YANG; one inlining to fix, not two. |\n| instruments | 65 non-test tools | 35 instruments behind 49 gates | different populations (all tools vs gate instruments) | Only for the \"Seventeen instruments\" fix: the replacement must say which population it counts, or derive it. |\n| theme-model readers | \"12 requirers\" | 12 non-test readers, 8 gates, 18 requirers total | YANG finer | Only for wording the replacement of \"Four gates\". |\n\n## A. The intent map\n\nEach group is one QUESTION. Inside a group, \"one shape\" means the members answer the question by\ndifferent means and should fuse; \"different grain\" names the axis on which they genuinely differ.\nSix axes come from the poles: population, scope, well-formedness, reader, grain, authority.\n\n### Q1. Does the grammar tokenize a given text as pinned?\n- 40 snapshots (737 distinct scopes, YIN) via snapshot-check.js — records what IS present; no syntax for absence (YANG).\n- 87 assertion fixtures (.tw5.test / .mem.test) — 1305 assertion lines over 415 distinct scopes (YIN); 36 carry negatives, 138 negatives total (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN from snapshots on well-formedness (absence) and on construct presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms appear in zero snapshots, YANG). Only 4 fixtures are behaviour-only (YANG): `blockquote`, `macro-string-params`, `mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines.\n- `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` — ONE SHAPE (one tool, one scope, two globs, populations 2 and 1; both poles concede).\n- `run_snapshots.sh`, `echo-test`, `list-test` — no reader (both). `tests-advanced/basic/htmltiddler/wikitext` — narrowings of one script (YIN), see F for the one check before striking them.\n- attic fixtures (6 YANG / 7 YIN), known-gaps (2), samples (1) — deliberately outside every glob, held by a README alone (YANG). Protected (E).\n- Follow YANG on grain: fixture ≠ snapshot. Follow both on snap-tid/snap-meta.\n\n### Q2. Where does the grammar disagree with the TW5 engine, and is each disagreement ruled?\n- `overreach` (31 exclusive rulings), `overreach-corpus-files` (57), `overreach-corpus-memetic` (2), `overreach-host` (2), `overreach-cut` (0); union 101 of 111; 10 unused = exactly the 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings keyed out by reader-scope.js (all YANG).\n- `expected-divergence` (the rulings), `divergence-staleness` (derives its run set FROM package.json — YIN A4 cites :48-51; YANG agrees), `reader-scope.js` (`readerOf`), `degraded-ground.test.js`.\n- DIFFERENT GRAIN on population: each arm is a different ground, and overreach-check.js:306 takes one pattern only (YIN A4) — the glob fusion is STRUCK by YIN itself. overreach-cut is the sole member with nothing exclusive; see C3.\n- Follow YANG's coupling measurement entirely; it is the strongest number in either report.\n\n### Q3. Does the engine compile the grammar, and does it behave as the model predicts?\n- `engine-witness` vs `engine-behaviour` — compile vs behaviour; engine-ledger's one ruling is BEHAVIOUR-prefixed and filtered out of the compile arm (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (well-formedness vs behaviour). Keep.\n- `theme-model` vs `theme-collision` — model vs the only reader of what vscode-textmate paints (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep.\n\n### Q4. How much of the corpus does the grammar read fully, and what remains unasked?\n- `still` (sampled, `--sample 25`, draw-dependent: seeds 1/2/9/13 green, seed 5 red on one tree, YANG) vs `still --reach` (whole; carrier floor seated at 0.9947; 23 of 4,403 carriers hold an unnamed class, so a draw of 25 misses all 23 seven times in eight, YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (population). Keep both.\n- `swallow-witness` — same 186 instances as still over the corpus (YIN A5), but alone produces `unasked-regions-ceiling` (97 regions, 83 open under some cut, 14 unasked, YIN; seated at 14, YANG) and an 18-region probe-escape reading. DIFFERENT QUESTION (coverage share vs region ceiling). Fusion STRUCK by YIN, defended by YANG — closed.\n- `carrier-reach-floor`, `coverage-floor`, `ruling-breadth-ceiling`, `still-breadth.js` (runs as a child process in sandboxes, YIN) — ratchets and helpers of this question. YANG names \"six coverage authorities\" without listing them; I cannot map the sixth and do not try.\n- `carrier-ledger` (7 rows) vs `swallow-ledger` — identical row grammar; carrier-ledger refuses fusion in its own header; `still --over ./corpus --reach` reads 52 of 52 carriers, 100.0%, floor 99.5%, without consulting the corpus ledger (YIN A3). Consistent with the header; not proven — wants the host sweep (F).\n\n### Q5. Does a construct take only its own scopes (containment)?\n- Five grains (YANG): `bleed-canary` / `composition-check` / `swallow-witness` / `memetic-containment` / `corpus-check`. Plus `canary-tid` (2 files) and `canary-memetic` (4) — what canary-memetic catches that memetic-containment does not: UNMEASURED (YANG, YIN did not take it up).\n- bleed-canary vs corpus-check containment: three sentinel STRINGS, two baseline derivations (oracle `readAt().kind === 'built'` vs a written control file) (YIN D2). ONE SHAPE for the strings; DIFFERENT GRAIN for the baselines (two of the five). Both poles now agree: share strings only.\n\n### Q6. Can anything reach a given scope?\n- `scope-can-appear` vs `grammar-reachability` vs `corpus-check` coverage — three causes of \"nothing reaches it\" (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep; no pole contested.\n\n### Q7. Is the grammar legible under the bundled themes?\n- `dark-construct` (absent rule) vs `contrast-witness` (rule within CIE76 ΔE of the default) vs `theme-parity` (below the median of six comparator grammars) — three MECHANISMS (YANG); 22 vs 26 findings, union 47, intersection 1, structurally exclusive per theme (YIN A1). DIFFERENT GRAIN. Keep all three; the duplicated anecdote (one event, three headers) gets ONE home. Both agree.\n- `colour-witness`, `colour-pair` (exists as both a ledger and a floor, YIN V1), `legibility-floor`, `prose-reading-ledger` (1 row) vs `PlainConstructs.tid` (20 keys) — intersection 0, split by FAULT is right, split by STORAGE is arbitrary (YIN A2). Keep both populations; the storage split is a Q-record question, not a collapse.\n- `theme-paint` / `family-atlas` / `page-palette` / `reading-recipe` — CLI surfaces may merge, questions may not; page-palette's question is read by nothing else (YANG). HELD (F).\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — the only implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). PROTECTED (E).\n- Theme population: node_modules/tm-themes hardcoded in five instruments, no override; 65 (both); `BAR = 16` literal vs \"a quarter of 65\" in prose (YIN R4).\n\n### Q8. Which rulings are owed, which hold, and what retires?\n- Six `/^OWED/` readings: ablation-witness:361, darkness-witness:219, swallow-witness:107-108 strip `READER <v>` via the shared reader; light-cone:81, bracket-witness:155, delimiter-inheritance.test:96,106 do not (YANG A3; YIN counted five sites, YANG six). ONE SHAPE: one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`.\n- Ledgers (12 ledger-shaped files, 10 named `-ledger`, YIN V1): ablation, darkness, swallow, bracket, carrier, engine, colour-pair, prose-reading, expected-divergence, must-fail, and the ones unnamed in the reports. Seven single-number ratchets, 4 floor / 3 ceiling; `attribute-kind-ceiling` holds a count that may FALL (direction misnamed once, YIN V1).\n- Lifecycle vocabulary: stale / explains nothing / retires / unruled / idle — one lifecycle, winner `idle` already at divergence-staleness:137 (YIN).\n- `MISS` is a homonym across two ledgers sharing one codec (YIN V3).\n- The two largest ledgers: ablation 359 rows / darkness 111 rows, 22 and 5 distinct causes (YANG) — see C4.\n\n### Q9. Does the harness guard itself?\n- `every-gate-collides` — MEASURED FALSE GREEN: name matched over raw text, clause over comment-stripped code (YANG; YIN B1 confirms lines 67/77 and calls it a GRAIN fault). Four CI gates outside it (`edition:check`, `lint-closure`, `package-contents`, `tests-known-gaps`); `package-contents.js` has no test and no collider (YANG). No invariant ever enters the pairing — exemption by construction, recorded nowhere (YIN B4: 21 of 39 carry a collider, 18 do not; YANG: 25 of 39 show no marker — different markers counted, neither matters once the exemption is written down).\n- `one-implementation` — fingerprint scan excludes `*.test.js` (POPULATION fault per YIN; a reasoned exclusion with a five-entry named exemption map per YANG). See C2.\n- `every-test-runs` — collects `*.test.js` only; all 96 fixtures outside it (YANG).\n- `gate-report --check` — NOT read-only: `ceiling` (textmate-ceiling.js --write) and `builtins` (builtins-sync.js without --check) write tracked files; both have --check arms the manifest does not use (YANG; YIN B2 names the law and two correct precedents: edition:build/check, signals/signals:check).\n- gate-report's SKIP regex vs a reasons list — two differently-keyed \"what is not a gate\" enumerations; the regex once hid two CI instruments (YIN R8). HELD (F).\n- CI: `compose` job is the only true duplicate (same ubuntu/node22 platform as `gates`, YIN B3); the grammar job's repeats are a four-way OS/node matrix; test.yml:88-95 re-running four gates is what makes them blocking (YANG) — PROTECTED (E). Dirty-tree guard (test.yml:60-69) runs in `grammar`, not `gates`.\n\n### Q10. Does the record match the tree?\n- Migration headline in four places; three hand-written restatement regexes; MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 say 111 where the certified figure is 110; LEDGER:1294 says 585/122/247 against 556/110/245 (YIN R1, R2; YANG concedes and adds \"one file contradicts itself 31 lines apart\").\n- \"65 bundled themes\" in 8 files, welded nowhere (YIN R3). 106 N/65-or-percent figures in the LEDGER, two welds, both reading the UNRELEASED section only (YANG). 6 `# checks:` machine twins in the whole repository, across 3 of 20 corpus files, none in the LEDGER (YIN).\n- README gate list: 25 distinct names, 3 duplicated bullets, 37 of 49 gates unnamed; the weld checks only that a named script exists (YIN R6).\n- Four wrong present-tense counts (both poles, all four confirmed by YANG): corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20); GateReport-Doc.tid \"Seventeen instruments\" (49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools); theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates, 12 non-test readers, 18 requirers); tokenizer.test.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test, 35 total).\n- Three colour-cluster header integers: reproduce in rank, direction and ratio, not in integer (YANG A2).\n- ONE SHAPE across all of these: a figure is either dated or derived. This is the mark (B, phase 3).\n\n### Q11. What ships?\n- `.vscodeignore`, `package-contents.js` (manifest → package only, never package → needed; YIN I, YANG 4). `vsce ls` shows 34 files: syntaxes/attic (14,013 + 936 bytes), docs/prior-art (seven documents, 280,996 bytes), nine shipped .mem documents + attic = 349,604 bytes (YIN A6, listing only, no extraction). `.vscodeignore:16` references run_snapshots.sh (YANG).\n\n### Q12. Does the grammar stall?\n- `backtrack-witness` — wall-clock: 1.4 ms alone vs past 8 ms under the suite, 0 vs 6 stalls between two runs at rest (YANG). Keep; every phase that changes what runs beside it must re-measure (F).\n\n## B. The ordered plan\n\nEach step: WHAT changes · WHAT is lost · MUST EXIST FIRST · EVIDENCE. No step depends on a later one.\n\"Collider\" means the `--must-fail` arm every gate owes under every-gate-collides.\n\n### Phase 0 — make the verifier honest (nothing is collapsed yet)\n\n**0.1 every-gate-collides strips comments before the NAME match** (the clause match already does).\nLost: nothing; at least one currently-green gate will red (YIN B1 cost asymmetry) — that red is the\nfinding, fix the collider it exposes, do not revert. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 1 (deleting\ntheme-collision.test.js's real `--must-fail` arm keeps the invariant green); corpus-ledgers.test.js:43\nis the worked example (YIN B1).\nWhy before step 1: every later step that moves or deletes a test file is certified by this invariant;\nstep 1 itself must carry delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107's collider across (YIN B3). A blind\ncollider check cannot certify that.\n\n**0.2 the manifest names only --check arms**: `ceiling` → textmate-ceiling.js --check,\n`builtins` → builtins-sync.js --check. Lost: nothing; the write arms stay as scripts for the hand.\nFirst: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 2; YIN B2 law (\"a harvest's CHECK arm belongs in the manifest,\nits WRITE arm never does\") and its two existing precedents.\nWhy here: the enacting hand runs `gate-report --check` after every phase; today that dirties the tree\ntwice per run and the dirty-tree guard sits in the other CI job.\n\n**0.3 write the exemption down**: one line in the LEDGER (or wherever rulings live) stating that\ninvariants enter no collider pairing by construction, and that the four CI gates outside the\ninvariant are listed by name. Lost: nothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 4–5, YIN B4.\nThis is a record act, but it must precede any step that would otherwise look like it removed a\ncollider obligation.\n\n### Phase 1 — the one latent fault that disarms a gate\n\n**1.1 one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()` in reader-scope.js; all six sites call it.**\nLost: nothing today — all 76 `READER … OWED` rows live in files whose readers already strip (YANG\nA3: 65/7/4). First: 0.1 (to certify the collider carried across). Evidence: YANG A3 — light-cone and\nswallow-witness read the SAME file with incompatible predicates; swallow-ledger holds five READER rows\nand zero OWED, and adding OWED to any one of them makes light-cone's one-sided gate read it as RULED\nand go silent. YIN D4 first named it; YANG promoted it to first.\nCollider: a `READER 5.4.1 OWED` row in a sandboxed swallow-ledger must red light-cone after the fix\nand must be shown green-by-silence before it (the RED-TESTS-FIRST control).\n\n**Do I agree with \"first\"?** Yes on substance: it precedes every collapse, because every rename of\na ledger (phase 4) and every re-keying of the backlog ledgers (C4) passes through this reader, and a\nre-key through six divergent predicates would be six faults. No on the literal position: phase 0 is\nwhat makes step 1.1's own verification trustworthy, and phase 0 costs three lines. If the hand must\npick a single step and stop, pick 1.1.\n\n### Phase 2 — pure removals (no record edit, no rename)\n\n**2.1 delete `run_snapshots.sh` + `echo-test` + `list-test` AND `.vscodeignore:16` together.**\nLost: nothing (zero readers, both poles). First: nothing. Evidence: YIN dead list; YANG's coupling\n(the package gate never reports a stale ignore entry).\n\n**2.2 strike the four narrowing test aliases** (82 → 75 scripts, YIN wave 2 — the 82 → 75 arithmetic\nis YIN's; 82 − 3 − 4 = 75 checks against 2.1). Lost: four names. First: the one check in F.1 (that the\numbrella script runs every population an alias ran, since every-test-runs does not see fixtures).\nEvidence: YIN; YANG concedes.\n\n**2.3 strike the CI `compose` job.** Lost: nothing (same platform as `gates`, YIN B3). First: nothing.\nMust NOT touch: test.yml:88-95 and the grammar job's matrix (E). Evidence: YIN B3; YANG concedes item 6.\n\n**2.4 fuse `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` into one script with two globs.** Lost: one line in the harvest\nsummary — YANG: the per-arm line is habit, no file states 49. First: 0.1; and read F.3 before\nenacting (a grep of the record for the literal gate count). Evidence: both concede; populations 2 and\n1; a different invariant holds the file associations (YANG).\n\n**2.5 the two real duplications the ward cannot see**: the byte-identical `scopesOn` in exactly two\ninvariants → tokenizer.js's helper; the one inlined `source ./grammars.sh` → the shared act; rename\nthe THIRD same-named different function (offset-based, returns an array — YANG A4). Lost: nothing.\nFirst: nothing — \"both fixable today without touching the ward\" (YANG). Evidence: YIN D8 counts\n(two helpers right), YANG's correction (one inlining, not two).\n\n**2.6 share the three sentinel STRINGS in sentinel.js; keep both baseline derivations.** Lost:\nnothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YIN D2 revised in wave 2; YANG \"strings only\".\n\n### Phase 3 — the mark, then every figure under it\n\n**3.1 define ONE syntax for a figure**: either `<figure> (read <date>, over <population>)` or a\nmachine-readable twin (the `# checks:` form already present in 6 places — reuse it, do not invent a\nsecond). Add ONE gate: a figure in a welded file carrying neither fails. Key the gate on the mark, NOT\non the UNRELEASED section (both existing welds go blind the day 3.1.0 opens — YANG).\nLost: nothing. First: nothing structural; but it comes AFTER phases 1–2 so the first derived figures\ndescribe the collapsed tree, and BEFORE 3.2–3.6 so each fix is an instance of the mark rather than a\none-time correction. Evidence: YANG's blind-spot section (\"arrived at from four directions\"); YIN R5\n(6 twins in the whole repository, none in 1840 LEDGER lines).\nWhere it sits in my order: third phase, gating all record work. YANG wanted it second; the only\ndifference is that I let the pure removals (phase 2) go first because they change counts the mark\nwould otherwise date twice.\n\n**3.2 one home for the migration headline** (556/110/245); the other three sites either derive it or\ncarry a date; fix MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 (111 → 110); mark LEDGER:1294's 585/122/247 as the\ndated sequence it narrates, do not \"correct\" it. The three restatement regexes become ONE weld keyed\non the mark. Evidence: YIN R1–R2; YANG concedes \"a correctness fix\".\nCaution (F.2): neither pole says whether the \"110\" certified migration figure and the \"111 rulings in\nexpected-divergence\" (YANG) count the same thing. Resolve before editing, or the fix may be wrong.\n\n**3.3 weld the theme count and derive BAR from it.** The weld reads \"65 as this repository bundles\nthem\" (five instruments hardcode node_modules/tm-themes, no override — YIN wave 2 wording); contrast-\nwitness's `BAR = 16` becomes a derivation from that count. Evidence: YIN R3–R4; YANG \"concede and\nWIDEN\".\n\n**3.4 fix the four wrong present-tense counts WITHOUT deleting the paragraphs** — each becomes either\nderived or dated: corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20), GateReport-Doc \"Seventeen instruments\"\n(say which population: 49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools, or defer to the manifest as the tool's own\nheader already does), theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates of 12 non-test readers), tokenizer.\ntest.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test of 35). Two of these headers are load-bearing prose (E); the number\nchanges, the law stays. Evidence: both poles, all four confirmed by YANG.\n\n**3.5 true still.js:35-38 (8 → 13 classes)**, dated. Evidence: YIN A5.\n\n**3.6 the three colour-cluster header integers get the date and population they were read over**\n(44 of 65; 1,191 of 11,756 = 10.1%; 18 of 54 = 33%). Do NOT overwrite with YANG's re-run (65 of 65;\n1,467 of 15,116 = 9.7%; 23 of 59 = 39%) — that would be a second undated reading; see C5. The shared\nanecdote (one event in three headers) moves to ONE home in the LEDGER, each header keeping one line\nthat names its MECHANISM. Evidence: YANG A2; YIN A1 (two lines of duplicated anecdote is all that\nsurvives of D3).\n\n**3.7 generate the README gate list's COMPLETENESS check; keep the curated prose; manifest gets only\nthe --check arm.** Evidence: YIN R6 revised; YANG concedes 9.\n\n**3.8 move gate-report.js:18-25 (the removed-flag archaeology) to LEDGER:1809-1812 which already\nholds it — a move, never a delete — and carry the 38-46 reader-keyed routing note's LAW with it in\nthe LEDGER while LEAVING the comment in place (no test holds it; YANG). Evidence: YIN 16 revised;\nYANG \"move, never lose\".\n\n### Phase 4 — vocabulary (one word per relation)\n\nEach rename passes through `owedOf`/`readerOf` and the ledger-shape module; hence after phase 1.\n\n**4.1 corpus file naming → `-ledger` / `-ratchet`, direction in the first comment line** (fixes the\none filename whose direction is wrong: `attribute-kind-ceiling` may FALL — YIN V1; confirm the\ncomparator before renaming, F.6). Collider obligations move with the names (YIN B4). Evidence: YIN\nV1; YANG concedes 13.\n**4.2 `MISS` → `dark` / `unmarked`** (one codec, two ledgers, one homonym). Evidence: YIN V3; YANG 14.\n**4.3 one lifecycle word**: `idle` (already at divergence-staleness:137) / `unruled` / `retired`.\nEvidence: YIN V4; YANG 15.\n**4.4 one spelling of over-reading** (OVERREACH / overbound / overreach — YIN V2). Nobody ranked it;\nit is a grep-and-replace once 4.1 settles the ledger names, with the weld test first\n(signing-domain grep per the house rule).\n\n### Phase 5 — conditional items (each has a named condition; do not enact without it)\n\n**5.1 syntaxes/attic (and docs/prior-art) out of the .vsix.** Condition: build and EXTRACT a .vsix\nfirst (YIN listed only); land WITH the first collider package-contents.js has ever had, and extend\npackage-contents to check package → needed, not only manifest → package. The attic STAYS IN THE TREE\nwith its README (E). Evidence: YIN A6; YANG 17 + fault 4.\n**5.2 retire `overreach-cut`.** Condition: degraded-ground.test.js resolves its TiddlyWiki host so it\nRUNS in CI (today it skips unless a checkout sits as a sibling — YANG B). Then the gate has 0 exclusive\nrulings and a byte-identical reader. See C3.\n**5.3 the backlog collapse (ablation 319 → 22 causes, darkness 92 → 5).** Condition, YANG's,\nnon-negotiable: the row grammar carries a `cause` identifier distinct from `key`; the breadth ratchet\ncounts distinct causes; a single-number ratchet seated at 1 refuses a ruling absorbing more than one\ncause, header stating why (the predecessor re-seated four times in one session: 55.8 → 56.6 → 57.2 →\n60.0%). See C4. After phase 1 and 4.1, since it re-keys rows every OWED reader parses.\n**5.4 fuse the four behaviour-only fixtures into snapshots** (`blockquote`, `macro-string-params`,\n`mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines, YANG). Condition: a snapshot sample exists that carries\neach construct (YANG confirmed for macro-string-params: 14 named-argument calls). Fix `blockquote`'s\nwrong header (\"Test for header syntaxes\") whether or not it fuses. See C1.\n**5.5 one home for judgements (corpus/*.txt vs JSON data tiddlers — YIN D5).** Condition: a stated\nreason per surface why it is text or tiddler. YIN A2 already found the storage split arbitrary for one\npair; nobody measured the other eight tiddlers. Record the axis; do not move data until it is read.\n**5.6 the four colour-reader CLI surfaces.** HELD. Condition: a reading of what page-palette answers\nthat nothing else does (YANG). No measurement exists; no step.\n**5.7 canary-memetic vs memetic-containment.** HELD; unmeasured by either pole.\n\n### Phase 6 — the Evergreen / L-Prime pass (section D), last, once.\n\n## C. The adjudications\n\n**C1. Fixture collapse (50 vs 60; vocabulary subsumption).**\nRuling: vocabulary subsumption is NOT a safe criterion, and the 50/60 gap is irrelevant to that.\nI follow YANG, on two measured properties: 36 of 87 fixtures carry 138 negative assertions and the\nsnapshot format has no syntax for absence (29 of the 60 would be lost outright); and construct\npresence ≠ vocabulary presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms in zero snapshots, three in\nzero broad-corpus files, one under a live OWED ruling). YIN itself flagged in wave 1 that its measure\nwas vocabulary not scope-at-position and handed the law question to YANG. Result: 4 fusible (5.4),\n83 stay. What would change it: a snapshot format that records absence AND a scope-at-position\nmeasurement showing the remaining fixtures add no position a snapshot lacks. Either alone is not enough.\n\n**C2. one-implementation's `*.test.js` exclusion.**\nRuling: keep the exclusion; extend the existing five-entry named-exemption map to the fingerprint scan\n(YANG A4). YIN's \"population fault\" reading was right that the ward cannot see two real duplications;\nYANG's reading of the reason two lines above the exclusion is decisive: a test must hold its gate's\ncollider, and the no-second-implementation rule applied to tests would forbid the collision the house\nrequires. Lifting the exclusion WIDENS obligations and needs an allowance list (YIN B1 concedes the\ncost). The two duplications are fixed in 2.5 without the ward. What would change it: if the exemption\nmap cannot express \"a test may carry its collided instrument's body\" — then write that rule as a new\nmap entry kind, still not a blanket lift.\n\n**C3. overreach-cut's CI reachability.**\nRuling: YANG's condition, in YANG's order. On the operator's machine degraded-ground.test.js runs the\ncut arm with a byte-identical invocation; in CI it skips, so the gate is the only reader there and a\nconditional skip reads as a pass (YANG fragile set). Fix the test's host resolution, confirm it runs in\nCI (a green that is a run, not a skip), then drop the script entry — and because divergence-staleness\nderives its run set from package.json, confirm the arm's exclusive count is still 0 at that moment\n(it was 0 over the current ruling file; it is not a property of the arm). What would change it: a\nnon-zero exclusive after the host fix, or no way to give CI a host — then the gate stays and its\nheader says why.\n\n**C4. The debt backlogs and the one-cause ratchet.**\nRuling: YANG's concession with YANG's condition. The two files hold 22 and 5 causes enumerated at\ncharacter-position grain because the key grammar forces one row per position (YANG, every reason\nread; darkness's largest cause, 89 rows, is stated again in the file's own header). They are an\nenumerated backlog, not 319 and 92 guards. The 330/93 vs 319/92 difference is method and does not\ntouch the ruling. The condition is non-negotiable because the only evidence we have of what happens\nwithout it is a ratchet re-seated four times in one session with every reason true. Order: after\nphase 1 (owedOf) and 4.1 (names), since it rewrites rows six readers parse. What would change it:\nevidence that some rows within one \"cause\" family are read individually by a gate (a row-grain reader\nnobody named) — then the family cannot collapse to one row.\n\n**C5. The colour cluster's irreproducible header integers.**\nRuling: they are dated readings in the present tense — YANG's own finding, \"one defect, not two\".\nRank, direction and ratio reproduce (YANG: variable first both times; quiet share 9.7% vs 10.1%);\nthe integers do not, and the population moved (59 vs 54 themes ruling `variable`; 15,116 vs 11,756\nrule-parts). Do not rewrite 44 → 65; date the 44 and name its population, or replace the integer with\na derivation that produces today's number as a machine twin. Three gates stay (three mechanisms, and\nYIN's union/intersection measurement makes fusion lose 46 of 47 findings). What would change it: a\nderivation script shipped with the module — then the live number replaces the dated one and the\nheader carries no integer at all.\n\n**Closed by the poles themselves, recorded here so no hand reopens them:** swallow ⊂ still (YIN\nstruck; stale \"8 classes\" was the cause); dark-construct ∪ contrast-witness (YIN struck: intersection\n1 of 47); the overreach glob fusion (YIN struck on overreach-check.js:306 and divergence-staleness's\nmanifest coupling); prose-reading-ledger ∪ PlainConstructs (YIN struck: intersection 0); the three\nflag-pairs as \"one tool two gates\" (withdrawn — flags switch POPULATION, not output, per YANG; YIN\naccepts).\n\n## D. The Evergreen / L-Prime pass, as work\n\nNumbers here are YIN's; YANG did not verify copula densities.\n\n**What the pass touches, in order of yield:**\n1. Harness comment archaeology (the record is MORE evergreen than the comments — YIN): gate-report.js:18-25\n   (moved in 3.8); coverage-floor's header (13 of 24 lines narrate two re-seats → one line stating the\n   current seat with its date, the two re-seats to the LEDGER); ruling-breadth-ceiling and must-fail\n   headers (same shape; YIN names them, gives no counts).\n2. The LEDGER: 127 copulas at 0.07/line, dominated by explanatory tails (\"which is\", \"is the\", \"is\n   what\"). Turn the tails; where a copula states a ruling, leave it and mark confidence. The 106\n   N-of-65 figures are handled by the mark (3.1), not by prose.\n3. README, MIGRATION, CHANGELOG at 0.04–0.06/line. The CHANGELOG is dated by construction — past\n   entries are readings with a date already; Evergreen touches only its UNRELEASED section. MIGRATION's\n   sentences that a weld regex reads change ONLY together with the weld (measure both sides).\n4. The four wrong-count paragraphs (3.4) — the number is fixed under the mark; the sentence around it\n   is turned to say what the count GUARDS (\"N instruments require this module, so a change here reaches\n   N gates\"), which is the L-Prime shape anyway.\n\n**What the pass must not touch:**\n- docs/scope-naming-prior-art.mem: 84 of its 87 copulas sit inside a VERBATIM handback (YIN). An\n  L-Prime pass there destroys the \"verbatim\" claim. Only the 3 outside the quotation are in scope.\n- contributing.md: zero copulas (YIN). Nothing to do; do not \"improve\" it.\n- The 19 load-bearing prose sites (section E): rephrase in place is allowed; moving, shortening or\n  deleting is not, unless the law is first carried into a test or a LEDGER row and the comment points at it.\n- Any sentence a weld regex reads (the three migration restatements, the README gate-list weld) —\n  change only with its weld.\n- Quoted error strings, operator-spoken rulings quoted as such, and fixture header lines that state a\n  law (YANG: 4 fixtures pin their law in the header line).\n\n**How a hand tells the difference, three questions per sentence:**\n1. Is it inside a fence, a quotation, or a block marked verbatim/handback? → leave it.\n2. Does an instrument read it (a weld regex, a `# checks:` twin, a header line a test greps)? → change\n   it only with that reader, and run the reader red first.\n3. Does it state a law nothing else holds (the E list, or any \"do not\" / \"refused\" / \"deferred\" with\n   no test beside it)? → keep the law's content word-for-word, turn only the copula, and never move it alone.\nIf all three answer no, turn it freely: name what the thing does, date any number, derive any count.\n\n## E. What I refuse to let a tidy-up touch\n\nEach entry: the thing · why it looks removable · the line to read first.\n- LEDGER:856-861 — the within-construct distinctness witness is DEFERRED BY RULING; looks like a gap. Read the row.\n- LEDGER:851-855 — the fragment left unarticulated on purpose; looks like an unfinished note.\n- LEDGER:780-790 — underline as one stroke; the ruling the uri-span scouts evidence.\n- LEDGER:836-850 — two REFUSED palette moves; look like TODOs.\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — look like dead scouts; the ONLY implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). No ward reads them; nothing else does either.\n- `still-breadth.js`, `tw5-oracle-path.js` — look unreferenced; run as child processes inside swapped-grammar sandboxes (YIN).\n- The shared modules (sentinel, region-kind, carrier-reading, ledger-shape, walk, jsonc, tokenizer, run-tool) — each header records the drift it prevents; they are the RESULTS of earlier collapses. one-implementation catches a re-expansion, not a wrong fusion (YANG).\n- tests/memetic-wikitext/attic/README.md and the 6-or-7 attic fixtures; syntaxes/attic/README.md — held by a README alone, invisible to every glob BY DESIGN (YANG). The attic may leave the PACKAGE (5.1), never the tree.\n- CI test.yml:88-95 — re-running four gates in the blocking job is what makes them blocking. A duplication reading un-blocks four gates silently (YANG). test.yml:60-69 — the dirty-tree guard's reason.\n- gate-report.js:38-46 — reader-keyed report routing, held by no test; and its SKIP regex (hid two CI instruments once — change only with the reasons list, F.5).\n- The 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings — \"unused\" only because reader-scope keys them out; 4 are OWED. Not stale; do not retire.\n- `overreach-corpus-memetic`'s 2 exclusive rulings — fusing that arm retires the ruling that makes the dialect readable (YANG).\n- engine-ledger's single BEHAVIOUR-prefixed ruling — filtered out of the compile arm on purpose.\n- carrier-ledger's header — refuses the swallow fusion in its own words; YIN's measurement is consistent with it.\n- `macro-dynamic-params` — 76 assertions, six of seven forms in zero snapshots, one under a live OWED ruling.\n- backtrack-witness.test.js:48-66; a-lar-uri-reads-without-underline's header; corpus-ledgers.test.js header (\"Thirteen\" — fix the number, keep the law); coverage-floor, ruling-breadth-ceiling, carrier-reach-floor, legibility-floor, colour-pair-ledger headers; delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107 (the collider owedOf must carry).\n- Conditional skips (checkout / seed / plugin absent) — they read as passes; do not convert a skip into a deletion of the gate it stands for.\n- The two baseline derivations in containment (oracle readAt vs written control file) — two of five grains, not two sentinels.\n- The three colour mechanisms' header lines naming the mechanism (one anecdote moves; three mechanisms stay).\n\n## F. What neither pole measured, and what would settle it\n\n1. **What runs the 96 fixtures** (every-test-runs sees none of them — YANG). Before 2.2 strikes the four\n   aliases: diff the fixture population each alias runs against the umbrella script's. Settles whether\n   an alias is a narrowing or the only runner of a family.\n2. **110 vs 111.** YIN's certified migration figure is 110; YANG counts 111 rulings in expected-divergence.\n   Same thing or two things? One grep of what the weld certifies. Settles whether 3.2 is a fix or a mistake.\n3. **Whether any file states the gate count** (\"49 of 49\" — YIN D; \"no file states 49\" — YANG). A grep for\n   the literal in the record. Settles the cost of 2.4 and 5.2.\n4. **`still --host --reach` over ~4,403 carriers** — settles carrier-ledger's 7 rows (YIN A3 is corpus-only).\n5. **The SKIP regex vs the reasons list** (YIN R8): which names the four CI gates outside the collider?\n   Settles whether 0.3 can key on one enumeration.\n6. **`attribute-kind-ceiling`'s comparator direction** — confirm the count may fall before 4.1 renames it.\n7. **The 63 `punctuation.separator` sites** — a fifth naming law or accretion (YIN V6/J). Settles whether\n   SCOPE-NAMES gains a rule or the sites get re-scoped; out of this plan's scope until read.\n8. **backtrack-witness under the collapsed suite** — re-measure after phases 2 and 5; the witness is\n   wall-clock and any change in what-runs-beside-what can move it (YANG).\n9. **The `# checks:` twin mechanism** — what reads the 6 twins today. Settles whether the mark (3.1) reuses\n   it or needs a second reader (it must not need a second).\n10. **A .vsix extracted, not listed** — settles 5.1's byte figures and whether docs/prior-art is wanted in\n    the package.\n11. **The four CLI colour surfaces and canary-memetic** — unmeasured by both; held until a reading exists.\n12. **Copula densities** — YIN's only; YANG did not verify. A second count before phase 6 is cheap.\n\n## G. The short form, for the hand\n\n0. Collider strips comments before the name match · manifest names only --check arms · write the invariants' exemption down.\n1. `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`, six sites, collider carried, red-first on a sandboxed swallow-ledger.\n2. Delete three dead scripts + `.vscodeignore:16` · strike four aliases (after F.1) · strike CI compose · fuse snap-tid/snap-meta · fix two duplications + one misnamed function · share three sentinel strings.\n3. Define the dated-or-derived mark with one gate keyed on the mark · then headline/110 (after F.2), theme count + BAR, four counts, 8→13, three colour integers dated, README check generated, gate-report archaeology moved.\n4. `-ledger`/`-ratchet` names with direction · MISS → dark/unmarked · idle/unruled/retired · one spelling of overreach.\n5. Conditionals, each behind its condition: attic out of the .vsix with package-contents' first collider · overreach-cut after degraded-ground runs in CI · backlogs after the cause field and the one-cause ratchet · four fixtures after their snapshot samples exist.\n6. L-Prime/Evergreen, once, with the three questions in D, never inside the verbatim handback.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfoCYWGasxZuTPrCE5G4Y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b77e10c-e27b-46a0-bcfb-6e3e679f26df","timestamp":"2026-10-07T16:37:41.581Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/VSCode-TW5-Syntax","sessionId":"7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9","version":"2.1.277","gitBranch":"release/3.0.0","slug":"luminous-strolling-torvalds"}
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/packages/lararium-sensorium/fixtures/claude-week-2026-10-07/sources/claude/7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9/subagents/agent-a876d2adbe9a4390a.jsonl:37:{"parentUuid":"8b77e10c-e27b-46a0-bcfb-6e3e679f26df","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6783d91d-7f3d-4a78-967a-2cee7de7078b","agentId":"a876d2adbe9a4390a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PQNGgLjVHWNM1XuEq9eCLk","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"06fcc3ae-84fc-462e-a68a-c18fbb525b22","timestamp":"2026-10-07T16:37:41.636Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md","content":"# Consolidation plan — VSCode-TW5-Syntax harness and record\n\nWritten blind, from four survey files only. Every number below belongs to the pole that measured it\n(YIN or YANG, wave 1 or 2); where I write no attribution, the two poles agree. I have not read the\nrepository and I invent no figure. The plan is written for the hand that enacts it; where a step\ndepends on a measurement nobody took, the step says so and section F names what would settle it.\n\n## 0. The four goals, kept apart\n\n| Goal | What it means for this plan | Where it lands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Isomorphic by intent | one shape per QUESTION; look-alikes that answer different questions stay, and the record says on which axis | section A |\n| YIN collapse | fewer parts; every survivor earns its place by a named reader or a named question | section B, phases 1–2, 4–5 |\n| Aftermath / Evergreen | the record states what holds; a dated reading carries its date; a live claim has a machine twin | section B, phase 3 (the mark) |\n| L-Prime | prose names what a thing DOES; copulas that remain carry a confidence marker | section B, phase 6, scoped in section D |\n\nThe order of phases matters: collapse before the record pass (so the record describes the collapsed\ntree once), the mark before any figure is \"corrected\" (so a corrected figure cannot drift again), and\nL-Prime last (so prose is touched once, after the moves).\n\n## 0.1 Where the poles' numbers differ, and which difference matters\n\n| Figure | YIN | YANG | Why they differ | Matters to the plan? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fixtures whose vocabulary a snapshot already carries | 50 of 87 | 60 of 87 | extraction width (YANG: \"YIN measured by a narrower extraction\") | No. Both poles reject vocabulary subsumption as the criterion (C1); the ruling rests on YANG's 56-pin-a-law reading, not on 50 or 60. |\n| OWED rows, ablation / darkness | 330 of 359 / 93 of 111 | 319 / 92 | line grep vs reason-splitting (YANG) | No. The distinct-cause counts (22 and 5, YANG, every row read) are what the backlog ruling (C4) uses, and YANG states they are robust to the method. Use YANG's row counts for the ~400-line reduction estimate, since they are the reason-split ones. |\n| container stacks resolving identically | tool header: 44 of 65 | YANG re-run: 65 of 65 | a dated header integer vs a fresh reading | Only as an INSTANCE of the dated-reading defect (C5). The load-bearing claim (\"a container-grain gate would be worthless\") holds under either. |\n| `READER … OWED` rows | 76, all in darkness-ledger | 76: 65 darkness, 7 ablation, 4 expected-divergence | YANG's split is finer | Yes, in one way: all three files are read by stripping readers (YANG), so NO live fault exists today; the latent fault sits in swallow-ledger (5 READER rows, 0 OWED, read by light-cone without stripping). Step 1 targets that file. |\n| fixture population | 76 + 11 (+7 attic); \"87–89\" | 96 (76 + 11 + 6 attic + 2 known-gaps + 1 in samples) | population definition; attic 7 vs 6 unverified by either | Yes for scoping: the fixture ruling covers the 87 shared .tw5/.mem fixtures; attic, known-gaps and samples are protected separately (section E). |\n| still.js:35-38 class count | header says 8; YIN measured 13 (summing to 186 = still's own 186) | — | stale comment | Yes: it is the comment that caused YIN to rank a destructive collapse first in wave 1. Fix under the mark (phase 3). |\n| `source ./grammars.sh` inlined | twice | once (the second file reads grammars.sh as a file, a different act) | YANG read the act, not the string | Yes: follow YANG; one inlining to fix, not two. |\n| instruments | 65 non-test tools | 35 instruments behind 49 gates | different populations (all tools vs gate instruments) | Only for the \"Seventeen instruments\" fix: the replacement must say which population it counts, or derive it. |\n| theme-model readers | \"12 requirers\" | 12 non-test readers, 8 gates, 18 requirers total | YANG finer | Only for wording the replacement of \"Four gates\". |\n\n## A. The intent map\n\nEach group is one QUESTION. Inside a group, \"one shape\" means the members answer the question by\ndifferent means and should fuse; \"different grain\" names the axis on which they genuinely differ.\nSix axes come from the poles: population, scope, well-formedness, reader, grain, authority.\n\n### Q1. Does the grammar tokenize a given text as pinned?\n- 40 snapshots (737 distinct scopes, YIN) via snapshot-check.js — records what IS present; no syntax for absence (YANG).\n- 87 assertion fixtures (.tw5.test / .mem.test) — 1305 assertion lines over 415 distinct scopes (YIN); 36 carry negatives, 138 negatives total (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN from snapshots on well-formedness (absence) and on construct presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms appear in zero snapshots, YANG). Only 4 fixtures are behaviour-only (YANG): `blockquote`, `macro-string-params`, `mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines.\n- `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` — ONE SHAPE (one tool, one scope, two globs, populations 2 and 1; both poles concede).\n- `run_snapshots.sh`, `echo-test`, `list-test` — no reader (both). `tests-advanced/basic/htmltiddler/wikitext` — narrowings of one script (YIN), see F for the one check before striking them.\n- attic fixtures (6 YANG / 7 YIN), known-gaps (2), samples (1) — deliberately outside every glob, held by a README alone (YANG). Protected (E).\n- Follow YANG on grain: fixture ≠ snapshot. Follow both on snap-tid/snap-meta.\n\n### Q2. Where does the grammar disagree with the TW5 engine, and is each disagreement ruled?\n- `overreach` (31 exclusive rulings), `overreach-corpus-files` (57), `overreach-corpus-memetic` (2), `overreach-host` (2), `overreach-cut` (0); union 101 of 111; 10 unused = exactly the 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings keyed out by reader-scope.js (all YANG).\n- `expected-divergence` (the rulings), `divergence-staleness` (derives its run set FROM package.json — YIN A4 cites :48-51; YANG agrees), `reader-scope.js` (`readerOf`), `degraded-ground.test.js`.\n- DIFFERENT GRAIN on population: each arm is a different ground, and overreach-check.js:306 takes one pattern only (YIN A4) — the glob fusion is STRUCK by YIN itself. overreach-cut is the sole member with nothing exclusive; see C3.\n- Follow YANG's coupling measurement entirely; it is the strongest number in either report.\n\n### Q3. Does the engine compile the grammar, and does it behave as the model predicts?\n- `engine-witness` vs `engine-behaviour` — compile vs behaviour; engine-ledger's one ruling is BEHAVIOUR-prefixed and filtered out of the compile arm (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (well-formedness vs behaviour). Keep.\n- `theme-model` vs `theme-collision` — model vs the only reader of what vscode-textmate paints (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep.\n\n### Q4. How much of the corpus does the grammar read fully, and what remains unasked?\n- `still` (sampled, `--sample 25`, draw-dependent: seeds 1/2/9/13 green, seed 5 red on one tree, YANG) vs `still --reach` (whole; carrier floor seated at 0.9947; 23 of 4,403 carriers hold an unnamed class, so a draw of 25 misses all 23 seven times in eight, YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (population). Keep both.\n- `swallow-witness` — same 186 instances as still over the corpus (YIN A5), but alone produces `unasked-regions-ceiling` (97 regions, 83 open under some cut, 14 unasked, YIN; seated at 14, YANG) and an 18-region probe-escape reading. DIFFERENT QUESTION (coverage share vs region ceiling). Fusion STRUCK by YIN, defended by YANG — closed.\n- `carrier-reach-floor`, `coverage-floor`, `ruling-breadth-ceiling`, `still-breadth.js` (runs as a child process in sandboxes, YIN) — ratchets and helpers of this question. YANG names \"six coverage authorities\" without listing them; I cannot map the sixth and do not try.\n- `carrier-ledger` (7 rows) vs `swallow-ledger` — identical row grammar; carrier-ledger refuses fusion in its own header; `still --over ./corpus --reach` reads 52 of 52 carriers, 100.0%, floor 99.5%, without consulting the corpus ledger (YIN A3). Consistent with the header; not proven — wants the host sweep (F).\n\n### Q5. Does a construct take only its own scopes (containment)?\n- Five grains (YANG): `bleed-canary` / `composition-check` / `swallow-witness` / `memetic-containment` / `corpus-check`. Plus `canary-tid` (2 files) and `canary-memetic` (4) — what canary-memetic catches that memetic-containment does not: UNMEASURED (YANG, YIN did not take it up).\n- bleed-canary vs corpus-check containment: three sentinel STRINGS, two baseline derivations (oracle `readAt().kind === 'built'` vs a written control file) (YIN D2). ONE SHAPE for the strings; DIFFERENT GRAIN for the baselines (two of the five). Both poles now agree: share strings only.\n\n### Q6. Can anything reach a given scope?\n- `scope-can-appear` vs `grammar-reachability` vs `corpus-check` coverage — three causes of \"nothing reaches it\" (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN (authority). Keep; no pole contested.\n\n### Q7. Is the grammar legible under the bundled themes?\n- `dark-construct` (absent rule) vs `contrast-witness` (rule within CIE76 ΔE of the default) vs `theme-parity` (below the median of six comparator grammars) — three MECHANISMS (YANG); 22 vs 26 findings, union 47, intersection 1, structurally exclusive per theme (YIN A1). DIFFERENT GRAIN. Keep all three; the duplicated anecdote (one event, three headers) gets ONE home. Both agree.\n- `colour-witness`, `colour-pair` (exists as both a ledger and a floor, YIN V1), `legibility-floor`, `prose-reading-ledger` (1 row) vs `PlainConstructs.tid` (20 keys) — intersection 0, split by FAULT is right, split by STORAGE is arbitrary (YIN A2). Keep both populations; the storage split is a Q-record question, not a collapse.\n- `theme-paint` / `family-atlas` / `page-palette` / `reading-recipe` — CLI surfaces may merge, questions may not; page-palette's question is read by nothing else (YANG). HELD (F).\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — the only implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). PROTECTED (E).\n- Theme population: node_modules/tm-themes hardcoded in five instruments, no override; 65 (both); `BAR = 16` literal vs \"a quarter of 65\" in prose (YIN R4).\n\n### Q8. Which rulings are owed, which hold, and what retires?\n- Six `/^OWED/` readings: ablation-witness:361, darkness-witness:219, swallow-witness:107-108 strip `READER <v>` via the shared reader; light-cone:81, bracket-witness:155, delimiter-inheritance.test:96,106 do not (YANG A3; YIN counted five sites, YANG six). ONE SHAPE: one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`.\n- Ledgers (12 ledger-shaped files, 10 named `-ledger`, YIN V1): ablation, darkness, swallow, bracket, carrier, engine, colour-pair, prose-reading, expected-divergence, must-fail, and the ones unnamed in the reports. Seven single-number ratchets, 4 floor / 3 ceiling; `attribute-kind-ceiling` holds a count that may FALL (direction misnamed once, YIN V1).\n- Lifecycle vocabulary: stale / explains nothing / retires / unruled / idle — one lifecycle, winner `idle` already at divergence-staleness:137 (YIN).\n- `MISS` is a homonym across two ledgers sharing one codec (YIN V3).\n- The two largest ledgers: ablation 359 rows / darkness 111 rows, 22 and 5 distinct causes (YANG) — see C4.\n\n### Q9. Does the harness guard itself?\n- `every-gate-collides` — MEASURED FALSE GREEN: name matched over raw text, clause over comment-stripped code (YANG; YIN B1 confirms lines 67/77 and calls it a GRAIN fault). Four CI gates outside it (`edition:check`, `lint-closure`, `package-contents`, `tests-known-gaps`); `package-contents.js` has no test and no collider (YANG). No invariant ever enters the pairing — exemption by construction, recorded nowhere (YIN B4: 21 of 39 carry a collider, 18 do not; YANG: 25 of 39 show no marker — different markers counted, neither matters once the exemption is written down).\n- `one-implementation` — fingerprint scan excludes `*.test.js` (POPULATION fault per YIN; a reasoned exclusion with a five-entry named exemption map per YANG). See C2.\n- `every-test-runs` — collects `*.test.js` only; all 96 fixtures outside it (YANG).\n- `gate-report --check` — NOT read-only: `ceiling` (textmate-ceiling.js --write) and `builtins` (builtins-sync.js without --check) write tracked files; both have --check arms the manifest does not use (YANG; YIN B2 names the law and two correct precedents: edition:build/check, signals/signals:check).\n- gate-report's SKIP regex vs a reasons list — two differently-keyed \"what is not a gate\" enumerations; the regex once hid two CI instruments (YIN R8). HELD (F).\n- CI: `compose` job is the only true duplicate (same ubuntu/node22 platform as `gates`, YIN B3); the grammar job's repeats are a four-way OS/node matrix; test.yml:88-95 re-running four gates is what makes them blocking (YANG) — PROTECTED (E). Dirty-tree guard (test.yml:60-69) runs in `grammar`, not `gates`.\n\n### Q10. Does the record match the tree?\n- Migration headline in four places; three hand-written restatement regexes; MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 say 111 where the certified figure is 110; LEDGER:1294 says 585/122/247 against 556/110/245 (YIN R1, R2; YANG concedes and adds \"one file contradicts itself 31 lines apart\").\n- \"65 bundled themes\" in 8 files, welded nowhere (YIN R3). 106 N/65-or-percent figures in the LEDGER, two welds, both reading the UNRELEASED section only (YANG). 6 `# checks:` machine twins in the whole repository, across 3 of 20 corpus files, none in the LEDGER (YIN).\n- README gate list: 25 distinct names, 3 duplicated bullets, 37 of 49 gates unnamed; the weld checks only that a named script exists (YIN R6).\n- Four wrong present-tense counts (both poles, all four confirmed by YANG): corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20); GateReport-Doc.tid \"Seventeen instruments\" (49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools); theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates, 12 non-test readers, 18 requirers); tokenizer.test.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test, 35 total).\n- Three colour-cluster header integers: reproduce in rank, direction and ratio, not in integer (YANG A2).\n- ONE SHAPE across all of these: a figure is either dated or derived. This is the mark (B, phase 3).\n\n### Q11. What ships?\n- `.vscodeignore`, `package-contents.js` (manifest → package only, never package → needed; YIN I, YANG 4). `vsce ls` shows 34 files: syntaxes/attic (14,013 + 936 bytes), docs/prior-art (seven documents, 280,996 bytes), nine shipped .mem documents + attic = 349,604 bytes (YIN A6, listing only, no extraction). `.vscodeignore:16` references run_snapshots.sh (YANG).\n\n### Q12. Does the grammar stall?\n- `backtrack-witness` — wall-clock: 1.4 ms alone vs past 8 ms under the suite, 0 vs 6 stalls between two runs at rest (YANG). Keep; every phase that changes what runs beside it must re-measure (F).\n\n## B. The ordered plan\n\nEach step: WHAT changes · WHAT is lost · MUST EXIST FIRST · EVIDENCE. No step depends on a later one.\n\"Collider\" means the `--must-fail` arm every gate owes under every-gate-collides.\n\n### Phase 0 — make the verifier honest (nothing is collapsed yet)\n\n**0.1 every-gate-collides strips comments before the NAME match** (the clause match already does).\nLost: nothing; at least one currently-green gate will red (YIN B1 cost asymmetry) — that red is the\nfinding, fix the collider it exposes, do not revert. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 1 (deleting\ntheme-collision.test.js's real `--must-fail` arm keeps the invariant green); corpus-ledgers.test.js:43\nis the worked example (YIN B1).\nWhy before step 1: every later step that moves or deletes a test file is certified by this invariant;\nstep 1 itself must carry delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107's collider across (YIN B3). A blind\ncollider check cannot certify that.\n\n**0.2 the manifest names only --check arms**: `ceiling` → textmate-ceiling.js --check,\n`builtins` → builtins-sync.js --check. Lost: nothing; the write arms stay as scripts for the hand.\nFirst: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 2; YIN B2 law (\"a harvest's CHECK arm belongs in the manifest,\nits WRITE arm never does\") and its two existing precedents.\nWhy here: the enacting hand runs `gate-report --check` after every phase; today that dirties the tree\ntwice per run and the dirty-tree guard sits in the other CI job.\n\n**0.3 write the exemption down**: one line in the LEDGER (or wherever rulings live) stating that\ninvariants enter no collider pairing by construction, and that the four CI gates outside the\ninvariant are listed by name. Lost: nothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YANG fault 4–5, YIN B4.\nThis is a record act, but it must precede any step that would otherwise look like it removed a\ncollider obligation.\n\n### Phase 1 — the one latent fault that disarms a gate\n\n**1.1 one `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()` in reader-scope.js; all six sites call it.**\nLost: nothing today — all 76 `READER … OWED` rows live in files whose readers already strip (YANG\nA3: 65/7/4). First: 0.1 (to certify the collider carried across). Evidence: YANG A3 — light-cone and\nswallow-witness read the SAME file with incompatible predicates; swallow-ledger holds five READER rows\nand zero OWED, and adding OWED to any one of them makes light-cone's one-sided gate read it as RULED\nand go silent. YIN D4 first named it; YANG promoted it to first.\nCollider: a `READER 5.4.1 OWED` row in a sandboxed swallow-ledger must red light-cone after the fix\nand must be shown green-by-silence before it (the RED-TESTS-FIRST control).\n\n**Do I agree with \"first\"?** Yes on substance: it precedes every collapse, because every rename of\na ledger (phase 4) and every re-keying of the backlog ledgers (C4) passes through this reader, and a\nre-key through six divergent predicates would be six faults. No on the literal position: phase 0 is\nwhat makes step 1.1's own verification trustworthy, and phase 0 costs three lines. If the hand must\npick a single step and stop, pick 1.1.\n\n### Phase 2 — pure removals (no record edit, no rename)\n\n**2.1 delete `run_snapshots.sh` + `echo-test` + `list-test` AND `.vscodeignore:16` together.**\nLost: nothing (zero readers, both poles). First: nothing. Evidence: YIN dead list; YANG's coupling\n(the package gate never reports a stale ignore entry).\n\n**2.2 strike the four narrowing test aliases** (82 → 75 scripts, YIN wave 2 — the 82 → 75 arithmetic\nis YIN's; 82 − 3 − 4 = 75 checks against 2.1). Lost: four names. First: the one check in F.1 (that the\numbrella script runs every population an alias ran, since every-test-runs does not see fixtures).\nEvidence: YIN; YANG concedes.\n\n**2.3 strike the CI `compose` job.** Lost: nothing (same platform as `gates`, YIN B3). First: nothing.\nMust NOT touch: test.yml:88-95 and the grammar job's matrix (E). Evidence: YIN B3; YANG concedes item 6.\n\n**2.4 fuse `snap-tid` + `snap-meta` into one script with two globs.** Lost: one line in the harvest\nsummary — YANG: the per-arm line is habit, no file states 49. First: 0.1; and read F.3 before\nenacting (a grep of the record for the literal gate count). Evidence: both concede; populations 2 and\n1; a different invariant holds the file associations (YANG).\n\n**2.5 the two real duplications the ward cannot see**: the byte-identical `scopesOn` in exactly two\ninvariants → tokenizer.js's helper; the one inlined `source ./grammars.sh` → the shared act; rename\nthe THIRD same-named different function (offset-based, returns an array — YANG A4). Lost: nothing.\nFirst: nothing — \"both fixable today without touching the ward\" (YANG). Evidence: YIN D8 counts\n(two helpers right), YANG's correction (one inlining, not two).\n\n**2.6 share the three sentinel STRINGS in sentinel.js; keep both baseline derivations.** Lost:\nnothing. First: nothing. Evidence: YIN D2 revised in wave 2; YANG \"strings only\".\n\n### Phase 3 — the mark, then every figure under it\n\n**3.1 define ONE syntax for a figure**: either `<figure> (read <date>, over <population>)` or a\nmachine-readable twin (the `# checks:` form already present in 6 places — reuse it, do not invent a\nsecond). Add ONE gate: a figure in a welded file carrying neither fails. Key the gate on the mark, NOT\non the UNRELEASED section (both existing welds go blind the day 3.1.0 opens — YANG).\nLost: nothing. First: nothing structural; but it comes AFTER phases 1–2 so the first derived figures\ndescribe the collapsed tree, and BEFORE 3.2–3.6 so each fix is an instance of the mark rather than a\none-time correction. Evidence: YANG's blind-spot section (\"arrived at from four directions\"); YIN R5\n(6 twins in the whole repository, none in 1840 LEDGER lines).\nWhere it sits in my order: third phase, gating all record work. YANG wanted it second; the only\ndifference is that I let the pure removals (phase 2) go first because they change counts the mark\nwould otherwise date twice.\n\n**3.2 one home for the migration headline** (556/110/245); the other three sites either derive it or\ncarry a date; fix MIGRATION:36 and SCOPE-NAMES:20 (111 → 110); mark LEDGER:1294's 585/122/247 as the\ndated sequence it narrates, do not \"correct\" it. The three restatement regexes become ONE weld keyed\non the mark. Evidence: YIN R1–R2; YANG concedes \"a correctness fix\".\nCaution (F.2): neither pole says whether the \"110\" certified migration figure and the \"111 rulings in\nexpected-divergence\" (YANG) count the same thing. Resolve before editing, or the fix may be wrong.\n\n**3.3 weld the theme count and derive BAR from it.** The weld reads \"65 as this repository bundles\nthem\" (five instruments hardcode node_modules/tm-themes, no override — YIN wave 2 wording); contrast-\nwitness's `BAR = 16` becomes a derivation from that count. Evidence: YIN R3–R4; YANG \"concede and\nWIDEN\".\n\n**3.4 fix the four wrong present-tense counts WITHOUT deleting the paragraphs** — each becomes either\nderived or dated: corpus-ledgers.test.js \"Thirteen\" (20), GateReport-Doc \"Seventeen instruments\"\n(say which population: 49 gates / 35 instruments / 65 tools, or defer to the manifest as the tool's own\nheader already does), theme-model.test.js \"Four gates\" (8 gates of 12 non-test readers), tokenizer.\ntest.js \"Twenty-two\" (26 non-test of 35). Two of these headers are load-bearing prose (E); the number\nchanges, the law stays. Evidence: both poles, all four confirmed by YANG.\n\n**3.5 true still.js:35-38 (8 → 13 classes)**, dated. Evidence: YIN A5.\n\n**3.6 the three colour-cluster header integers get the date and population they were read over**\n(44 of 65; 1,191 of 11,756 = 10.1%; 18 of 54 = 33%). Do NOT overwrite with YANG's re-run (65 of 65;\n1,467 of 15,116 = 9.7%; 23 of 59 = 39%) — that would be a second undated reading; see C5. The shared\nanecdote (one event in three headers) moves to ONE home in the LEDGER, each header keeping one line\nthat names its MECHANISM. Evidence: YANG A2; YIN A1 (two lines of duplicated anecdote is all that\nsurvives of D3).\n\n**3.7 generate the README gate list's COMPLETENESS check; keep the curated prose; manifest gets only\nthe --check arm.** Evidence: YIN R6 revised; YANG concedes 9.\n\n**3.8 move gate-report.js:18-25 (the removed-flag archaeology) to LEDGER:1809-1812 which already\nholds it — a move, never a delete — and carry the 38-46 reader-keyed routing note's LAW with it in\nthe LEDGER while LEAVING the comment in place (no test holds it; YANG). Evidence: YIN 16 revised;\nYANG \"move, never lose\".\n\n### Phase 4 — vocabulary (one word per relation)\n\nEach rename passes through `owedOf`/`readerOf` and the ledger-shape module; hence after phase 1.\n\n**4.1 corpus file naming → `-ledger` / `-ratchet`, direction in the first comment line** (fixes the\none filename whose direction is wrong: `attribute-kind-ceiling` may FALL — YIN V1; confirm the\ncomparator before renaming, F.6). Collider obligations move with the names (YIN B4). Evidence: YIN\nV1; YANG concedes 13.\n**4.2 `MISS` → `dark` / `unmarked`** (one codec, two ledgers, one homonym). Evidence: YIN V3; YANG 14.\n**4.3 one lifecycle word**: `idle` (already at divergence-staleness:137) / `unruled` / `retired`.\nEvidence: YIN V4; YANG 15.\n**4.4 one spelling of over-reading** (OVERREACH / overbound / overreach — YIN V2). Nobody ranked it;\nit is a grep-and-replace once 4.1 settles the ledger names, with the weld test first\n(signing-domain grep per the house rule).\n\n### Phase 5 — conditional items (each has a named condition; do not enact without it)\n\n**5.1 syntaxes/attic (and docs/prior-art) out of the .vsix.** Condition: build and EXTRACT a .vsix\nfirst (YIN listed only); land WITH the first collider package-contents.js has ever had, and extend\npackage-contents to check package → needed, not only manifest → package. The attic STAYS IN THE TREE\nwith its README (E). Evidence: YIN A6; YANG 17 + fault 4.\n**5.2 retire `overreach-cut`.** Condition: degraded-ground.test.js resolves its TiddlyWiki host so it\nRUNS in CI (today it skips unless a checkout sits as a sibling — YANG B). Then the gate has 0 exclusive\nrulings and a byte-identical reader. See C3.\n**5.3 the backlog collapse (ablation 319 → 22 causes, darkness 92 → 5).** Condition, YANG's,\nnon-negotiable: the row grammar carries a `cause` identifier distinct from `key`; the breadth ratchet\ncounts distinct causes; a single-number ratchet seated at 1 refuses a ruling absorbing more than one\ncause, header stating why (the predecessor re-seated four times in one session: 55.8 → 56.6 → 57.2 →\n60.0%). See C4. After phase 1 and 4.1, since it re-keys rows every OWED reader parses.\n**5.4 fuse the four behaviour-only fixtures into snapshots** (`blockquote`, `macro-string-params`,\n`mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines, YANG). Condition: a snapshot sample exists that carries\neach construct (YANG confirmed for macro-string-params: 14 named-argument calls). Fix `blockquote`'s\nwrong header (\"Test for header syntaxes\") whether or not it fuses. See C1.\n**5.5 one home for judgements (corpus/*.txt vs JSON data tiddlers — YIN D5).** Condition: a stated\nreason per surface why it is text or tiddler. YIN A2 already found the storage split arbitrary for one\npair; nobody measured the other eight tiddlers. Record the axis; do not move data until it is read.\n**5.6 the four colour-reader CLI surfaces.** HELD. Condition: a reading of what page-palette answers\nthat nothing else does (YANG). No measurement exists; no step.\n**5.7 canary-memetic vs memetic-containment.** HELD; unmeasured by either pole.\n\n### Phase 6 — the Evergreen / L-Prime pass (section D), last, once.\n\n## C. The adjudications\n\n**C1. Fixture collapse (50 vs 60; vocabulary subsumption).**\nRuling: vocabulary subsumption is NOT a safe criterion, and the 50/60 gap is irrelevant to that.\nI follow YANG, on two measured properties: 36 of 87 fixtures carry 138 negative assertions and the\nsnapshot format has no syntax for absence (29 of the 60 would be lost outright); and construct\npresence ≠ vocabulary presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms in zero snapshots, three in\nzero broad-corpus files, one under a live OWED ruling). YIN itself flagged in wave 1 that its measure\nwas vocabulary not scope-at-position and handed the law question to YANG. Result: 4 fusible (5.4),\n83 stay. What would change it: a snapshot format that records absence AND a scope-at-position\nmeasurement showing the remaining fixtures add no position a snapshot lacks. Either alone is not enough.\n\n**C2. one-implementation's `*.test.js` exclusion.**\nRuling: keep the exclusion; extend the existing five-entry named-exemption map to the fingerprint scan\n(YANG A4). YIN's \"population fault\" reading was right that the ward cannot see two real duplications;\nYANG's reading of the reason two lines above the exclusion is decisive: a test must hold its gate's\ncollider, and the no-second-implementation rule applied to tests would forbid the collision the house\nrequires. Lifting the exclusion WIDENS obligations and needs an allowance list (YIN B1 concedes the\ncost). The two duplications are fixed in 2.5 without the ward. What would change it: if the exemption\nmap cannot express \"a test may carry its collided instrument's body\" — then write that rule as a new\nmap entry kind, still not a blanket lift.\n\n**C3. overreach-cut's CI reachability.**\nRuling: YANG's condition, in YANG's order. On the operator's machine degraded-ground.test.js runs the\ncut arm with a byte-identical invocation; in CI it skips, so the gate is the only reader there and a\nconditional skip reads as a pass (YANG fragile set). Fix the test's host resolution, confirm it runs in\nCI (a green that is a run, not a skip), then drop the script entry — and because divergence-staleness\nderives its run set from package.json, confirm the arm's exclusive count is still 0 at that moment\n(it was 0 over the current ruling file; it is not a property of the arm). What would change it: a\nnon-zero exclusive after the host fix, or no way to give CI a host — then the gate stays and its\nheader says why.\n\n**C4. The debt backlogs and the one-cause ratchet.**\nRuling: YANG's concession with YANG's condition. The two files hold 22 and 5 causes enumerated at\ncharacter-position grain because the key grammar forces one row per position (YANG, every reason\nread; darkness's largest cause, 89 rows, is stated again in the file's own header). They are an\nenumerated backlog, not 319 and 92 guards. The 330/93 vs 319/92 difference is method and does not\ntouch the ruling. The condition is non-negotiable because the only evidence we have of what happens\nwithout it is a ratchet re-seated four times in one session with every reason true. Order: after\nphase 1 (owedOf) and 4.1 (names), since it rewrites rows six readers parse. What would change it:\nevidence that some rows within one \"cause\" family are read individually by a gate (a row-grain reader\nnobody named) — then the family cannot collapse to one row.\n\n**C5. The colour cluster's irreproducible header integers.**\nRuling: they are dated readings in the present tense — YANG's own finding, \"one defect, not two\".\nRank, direction and ratio reproduce (YANG: variable first both times; quiet share 9.7% vs 10.1%);\nthe integers do not, and the population moved (59 vs 54 themes ruling `variable`; 15,116 vs 11,756\nrule-parts). Do not rewrite 44 → 65; date the 44 and name its population, or replace the integer with\na derivation that produces today's number as a machine twin. Three gates stay (three mechanisms, and\nYIN's union/intersection measurement makes fusion lose 46 of 47 findings). What would change it: a\nderivation script shipped with the module — then the live number replaces the dated one and the\nheader carries no integer at all.\n\n**Closed by the poles themselves, recorded here so no hand reopens them:** swallow ⊂ still (YIN\nstruck; stale \"8 classes\" was the cause); dark-construct ∪ contrast-witness (YIN struck: intersection\n1 of 47); the overreach glob fusion (YIN struck on overreach-check.js:306 and divergence-staleness's\nmanifest coupling); prose-reading-ledger ∪ PlainConstructs (YIN struck: intersection 0); the three\nflag-pairs as \"one tool two gates\" (withdrawn — flags switch POPULATION, not output, per YANG; YIN\naccepts).\n\n## D. The Evergreen / L-Prime pass, as work\n\nNumbers here are YIN's; YANG did not verify copula densities.\n\n**What the pass touches, in order of yield:**\n1. Harness comment archaeology (the record is MORE evergreen than the comments — YIN): gate-report.js:18-25\n   (moved in 3.8); coverage-floor's header (13 of 24 lines narrate two re-seats → one line stating the\n   current seat with its date, the two re-seats to the LEDGER); ruling-breadth-ceiling and must-fail\n   headers (same shape; YIN names them, gives no counts).\n2. The LEDGER: 127 copulas at 0.07/line, dominated by explanatory tails (\"which is\", \"is the\", \"is\n   what\"). Turn the tails; where a copula states a ruling, leave it and mark confidence. The 106\n   N-of-65 figures are handled by the mark (3.1), not by prose.\n3. README, MIGRATION, CHANGELOG at 0.04–0.06/line. The CHANGELOG is dated by construction — past\n   entries are readings with a date already; Evergreen touches only its UNRELEASED section. MIGRATION's\n   sentences that a weld regex reads change ONLY together with the weld (measure both sides).\n4. The four wrong-count paragraphs (3.4) — the number is fixed under the mark; the sentence around it\n   is turned to say what the count GUARDS (\"N instruments require this module, so a change here reaches\n   N gates\"), which is the L-Prime shape anyway.\n\n**What the pass must not touch:**\n- docs/scope-naming-prior-art.mem: 84 of its 87 copulas sit inside a VERBATIM handback (YIN). An\n  L-Prime pass there destroys the \"verbatim\" claim. Only the 3 outside the quotation are in scope.\n- contributing.md: zero copulas (YIN). Nothing to do; do not \"improve\" it.\n- The 19 load-bearing prose sites (section E): rephrase in place is allowed; moving, shortening or\n  deleting is not, unless the law is first carried into a test or a LEDGER row and the comment points at it.\n- Any sentence a weld regex reads (the three migration restatements, the README gate-list weld) —\n  change only with its weld.\n- Quoted error strings, operator-spoken rulings quoted as such, and fixture header lines that state a\n  law (YANG: 4 fixtures pin their law in the header line).\n\n**How a hand tells the difference, three questions per sentence:**\n1. Is it inside a fence, a quotation, or a block marked verbatim/handback? → leave it.\n2. Does an instrument read it (a weld regex, a `# checks:` twin, a header line a test greps)? → change\n   it only with that reader, and run the reader red first.\n3. Does it state a law nothing else holds (the E list, or any \"do not\" / \"refused\" / \"deferred\" with\n   no test beside it)? → keep the law's content word-for-word, turn only the copula, and never move it alone.\nIf all three answer no, turn it freely: name what the thing does, date any number, derive any count.\n\n## E. What I refuse to let a tidy-up touch\n\nEach entry: the thing · why it looks removable · the line to read first.\n- LEDGER:856-861 — the within-construct distinctness witness is DEFERRED BY RULING; looks like a gap. Read the row.\n- LEDGER:851-855 — the fragment left unarticulated on purpose; looks like an unfinished note.\n- LEDGER:780-790 — underline as one stroke; the ruling the uri-span scouts evidence.\n- LEDGER:836-850 — two REFUSED palette moves; look like TODOs.\n- `uri-span-measure` / `uri-span-compare` — look like dead scouts; the ONLY implementation of the continuity reading, cited by name for the underline ruling (YANG). No ward reads them; nothing else does either.\n- `still-breadth.js`, `tw5-oracle-path.js` — look unreferenced; run as child processes inside swapped-grammar sandboxes (YIN).\n- The shared modules (sentinel, region-kind, carrier-reading, ledger-shape, walk, jsonc, tokenizer, run-tool) — each header records the drift it prevents; they are the RESULTS of earlier collapses. one-implementation catches a re-expansion, not a wrong fusion (YANG).\n- tests/memetic-wikitext/attic/README.md and the 6-or-7 attic fixtures; syntaxes/attic/README.md — held by a README alone, invisible to every glob BY DESIGN (YANG). The attic may leave the PACKAGE (5.1), never the tree.\n- CI test.yml:88-95 — re-running four gates in the blocking job is what makes them blocking. A duplication reading un-blocks four gates silently (YANG). test.yml:60-69 — the dirty-tree guard's reason.\n- gate-report.js:38-46 — reader-keyed report routing, held by no test; and its SKIP regex (hid two CI instruments once — change only with the reasons list, F.5).\n- The 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings — \"unused\" only because reader-scope keys them out; 4 are OWED. Not stale; do not retire.\n- `overreach-corpus-memetic`'s 2 exclusive rulings — fusing that arm retires the ruling that makes the dialect readable (YANG).\n- engine-ledger's single BEHAVIOUR-prefixed ruling — filtered out of the compile arm on purpose.\n- carrier-ledger's header — refuses the swallow fusion in its own words; YIN's measurement is consistent with it.\n- `macro-dynamic-params` — 76 assertions, six of seven forms in zero snapshots, one under a live OWED ruling.\n- backtrack-witness.test.js:48-66; a-lar-uri-reads-without-underline's header; corpus-ledgers.test.js header (\"Thirteen\" — fix the number, keep the law); coverage-floor, ruling-breadth-ceiling, carrier-reach-floor, legibility-floor, colour-pair-ledger headers; delimiter-inheritance.test.js:104-107 (the collider owedOf must carry).\n- Conditional skips (checkout / seed / plugin absent) — they read as passes; do not convert a skip into a deletion of the gate it stands for.\n- The two baseline derivations in containment (oracle readAt vs written control file) — two of five grains, not two sentinels.\n- The three colour mechanisms' header lines naming the mechanism (one anecdote moves; three mechanisms stay).\n\n## F. What neither pole measured, and what would settle it\n\n1. **What runs the 96 fixtures** (every-test-runs sees none of them — YANG). Before 2.2 strikes the four\n   aliases: diff the fixture population each alias runs against the umbrella script's. Settles whether\n   an alias is a narrowing or the only runner of a family.\n2. **110 vs 111.** YIN's certified migration figure is 110; YANG counts 111 rulings in expected-divergence.\n   Same thing or two things? One grep of what the weld certifies. Settles whether 3.2 is a fix or a mistake.\n3. **Whether any file states the gate count** (\"49 of 49\" — YIN D; \"no file states 49\" — YANG). A grep for\n   the literal in the record. Settles the cost of 2.4 and 5.2.\n4. **`still --host --reach` over ~4,403 carriers** — settles carrier-ledger's 7 rows (YIN A3 is corpus-only).\n5. **The SKIP regex vs the reasons list** (YIN R8): which names the four CI gates outside the collider?\n   Settles whether 0.3 can key on one enumeration.\n6. **`attribute-kind-ceiling`'s comparator direction** — confirm the count may fall before 4.1 renames it.\n7. **The 63 `punctuation.separator` sites** — a fifth naming law or accretion (YIN V6/J). Settles whether\n   SCOPE-NAMES gains a rule or the sites get re-scoped; out of this plan's scope until read.\n8. **backtrack-witness under the collapsed suite** — re-measure after phases 2 and 5; the witness is\n   wall-clock and any change in what-runs-beside-what can move it (YANG).\n9. **The `# checks:` twin mechanism** — what reads the 6 twins today. Settles whether the mark (3.1) reuses\n   it or needs a second reader (it must not need a second).\n10. **A .vsix extracted, not listed** — settles 5.1's byte figures and whether docs/prior-art is wanted in\n    the package.\n11. **The four CLI colour surfaces and canary-memetic** — unmeasured by both; held until a reading exists.\n12. **Copula densities** — YIN's only; YANG did not verify. A second count before phase 6 is cheap.\n\n## G. The short form, for the hand\n\n0. Collider strips comments before the name match · manifest names only --check arms · write the invariants' exemption down.\n1. `owedOf()` beside `readerOf()`, six sites, collider carried, red-first on a sandboxed swallow-ledger.\n2. Delete three dead scripts + `.vscodeignore:16` · strike four aliases (after F.1) · strike CI compose · fuse snap-tid/snap-meta · fix two duplications + one misnamed function · share three sentinel strings.\n3. Define the dated-or-derived mark with one gate keyed on the mark · then headline/110 (after F.2), theme count + BAR, four counts, 8→13, three colour integers dated, README check generated, gate-report archaeology moved.\n4. `-ledger`/`-ratchet` names with direction · MISS → dark/unmarked · idle/unruled/retired · one spelling of overreach.\n5. Conditionals, each behind its condition: attic out of the .vsix with package-contents' first collider · overreach-cut after degraded-ground runs in CI · backlogs after the cause field and the one-cause ratchet · four fixtures after their snapshot samples exist.\n6. L-Prime/Evergreen, once, with the three questions in D, never inside the verbatim handback.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b77e10c-e27b-46a0-bcfb-6e3e679f26df","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/VSCode-TW5-Syntax","sessionId":"7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9","version":"2.1.277","gitBranch":"release/3.0.0","slug":"luminous-strolling-torvalds"}
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/packages/lararium-sensorium/fixtures/claude-week-2026-10-07/sources/claude/7bdba225-5131-4cfb-a258-6d3e7da875f9/subagents/agent-a876d2adbe9a4390a.jsonl:42:{"parentUuid":"0785d956-75fc-4bb0-924a-c05c944b8d0f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a876d2adbe9a4390a","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfoCpJ3ZPqXmRZjnAjGcm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LjGH8R24htp3yfgT1VLBdd","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Consolidation plan saved to `/home/joshu/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/fable/consolidation-plan.md`. Nothing in the repository was read, run, or edited; the plan stands on the four survey files alone. Full text follows.\n\n---\n\n# Consolidation plan — VSCode-TW5-Syntax harness and record\n\nWritten blind, from four survey files only. Every number below belongs to the pole that measured it (YIN or YANG, wave 1 or 2); where I write no attribution, the two poles agree. I have not read the repository and I invent no figure. Where a step depends on a measurement nobody took, the step says so and section F names what would settle it.\n\n## 0. The four goals, kept apart\n\n| Goal | What it means here | Where it lands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Isomorphic by intent | one shape per QUESTION; look-alikes answering different questions stay, and the record says on which axis | A |\n| YIN collapse | fewer parts; every survivor earns its place by a named reader or question | B phases 1–2, 4–5 |\n| Aftermath / Evergreen | a dated reading carries its date; a live claim has a machine twin | B phase 3 (the mark) |\n| L-Prime | prose names what a thing DOES; remaining copulas carry a confidence marker | B phase 6, scoped in D |\n\nOrder matters: collapse before the record pass (so the record describes the collapsed tree once), the mark before any figure is \"corrected\" (so a corrected figure cannot drift again), L-Prime last (so prose is touched once, after the moves).\n\n## 0.1 Where the poles' numbers differ, and which difference matters\n\n| Figure | YIN | YANG | Why | Matters? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fixtures whose vocabulary a snapshot carries | 50 of 87 | 60 of 87 | extraction width | No — both reject vocabulary subsumption as the criterion (C1); the ruling rests on YANG's 56-pin-a-law reading. |\n| OWED rows ablation / darkness | 330/359, 93/111 | 319, 92 | line grep vs reason-split | No — the distinct-cause counts (22 and 5, YANG, every row read) drive C4 and are robust to method. Use YANG's rows for the ~400-line estimate. |\n| container stacks identical | header 44/65 | YANG re-run 65/65 | dated header integer vs fresh reading | Only as an instance of the dated-reading defect (C5); the load-bearing claim holds either way. |\n| `READER … OWED` rows | 76, all darkness | 76: 65 darkness, 7 ablation, 4 expected-divergence | YANG finer | Yes — all three files are read by stripping readers, so NO live fault today; the latent fault is swallow-ledger (5 READER rows, 0 OWED, read by light-cone without stripping). Step 1 targets that file. |\n| fixture population | 76+11(+7 attic), \"87–89\" | 96 (76+11+6 attic+2 known-gaps+1 samples) | population definition; attic 7 vs 6 unverified | Yes for scope: the fixture ruling covers the 87 shared; attic/known-gaps/samples are protected separately (E). |\n| still.js:35-38 class count | header 8; YIN measured 13 (summing to 186 = still's 186) | — | stale comment | Yes — it caused YIN to rank a destructive collapse first in wave 1. Fix under the mark. |\n| `source ./grammars.sh` inlined | twice | once (the other file reads it as a file) | YANG read the act | Yes — follow YANG; one to fix. |\n| instruments | 65 non-test tools | 35 instruments / 49 gates | different populations | Only for the \"Seventeen instruments\" fix: say which population, or derive. |\n| theme-model readers | 12 requirers | 12 non-test readers, 8 gates, 18 requirers | YANG finer | Only for wording \"Four gates\". |\n\n## A. The intent map\n\nSix axes from the poles: population, scope, well-formedness, reader, grain, authority.\n\n**Q1. Does the grammar tokenize a text as pinned?** 40 snapshots (737 scopes, YIN) — records presence only, no syntax for absence (YANG). 87 fixtures — 1305 lines / 415 scopes (YIN); 36 carry negatives, 138 total (YANG). DIFFERENT GRAIN from snapshots on absence and on construct presence (six of seven `macro-dynamic-params` forms in zero snapshots, YANG). Only 4 behaviour-only fixtures (YANG): `blockquote`, `macro-string-params`, `mvv-display`, `pragma-params-mvv`, 127 lines. `snap-tid`+`snap-meta` — ONE SHAPE (both concede). `run_snapshots.sh`, `echo-test`, `list-test` — no reader. The four `tests-*` aliases — narrowings (see F.1). Attic (6/7), known-gaps (2), samples (1) — deliberately outside every glob (YANG), protected.\n\n**Q2. Where does the grammar disagree with the TW5 engine, and is each disagreement ruled?** `overreach` 31 exclusives · `-corpus-files` 57 · `-corpus-memetic` 2 · `-host` 2 · `-cut` 0; union 101 of 111; 10 unused = exactly the 10 `READER 5.5.0-prerelease` rulings keyed out by reader-scope.js (all YANG). `expected-divergence`, `divergence-staleness` (run set FROM package.json — YIN A4 :48-51), `reader-scope.js`, `degraded-ground.test.js`. DIFFERENT GRAIN on population; overreach-check.js:306 takes one pattern only, so the glob fusion is STRUCK by YIN itself. overreach-cut: C3.\n\n**Q3. Does the engine compile it, and behave as the model predicts?** `engine-witness` vs `engine-behaviour` (compile vs behaviour; the one engine-ledger ruling is BEHAVIOUR-prefixed, YANG) — keep. `theme-model` vs `theme-collision` (model vs the only reader of what vscode-textmate paints) — keep.\n\n**Q4. How much corpus is read fully; what remains unasked?** `still` (sampled; seeds 1/2/9/13 green, 5 red on one tree) vs `still --reach` (whole; floor 0.9947; 23 of 4,403 carriers unnamed, so a draw of 25 misses all 23 seven times in eight — YANG). Keep both. `swallow-witness` — same 186 instances as still (YIN A5) but alone yields `unasked-regions-ceiling` (97 regions, 83 open under some cut, 14 unasked) and an 18-region probe-escape reading. DIFFERENT QUESTION; fusion struck by YIN, defended by YANG — closed. `carrier-reach-floor`, `coverage-floor`, `ruling-breadth-ceiling`, `still-breadth.js` (child process in sandboxes). YANG names \"six coverage authorities\" without listing them; I do not map the sixth. `carrier-ledger` (7 rows) vs `swallow-ledger`: identical row grammar; header refuses fusion; `still --over ./corpus --reach` reads 52/52, 100.0%, floor 99.5%, never consulting the corpus ledger (YIN A3) — consistent, not proven (F.4).\n\n**Q5. Containment.** Five grains (YANG): bleed-canary / composition-check / swallow-witness / memetic-containment / corpus-check; plus canary-tid (2) and canary-memetic (4) — unmeasured against memetic-containment. bleed-canary vs corpus-check: three sentinel STRINGS, two baseline derivations (oracle `readAt().kind==='built'` vs a written control file). ONE SHAPE for strings; DIFFERENT GRAIN for baselines. Both agree: share strings only.\n\n**Q6. Can anything reach a scope?** scope-can-appear / grammar-reachability / corpus-check coverage — three causes (YANG). Keep; uncontested.\n\n**Q7. Legibility under themes.** dark-construct (absent rule) / contrast-witness (within CIE76 ΔE of default) / theme-parity (below the median of six comparators) — three MECHANISMS (YANG); 22 vs 26 findings, union 47, intersection 1 (YIN A1). Keep three; one home for the anecdote. colour-witness, colour-pair (ledger AND floor), legibility-floor; prose-reading-ledger (1 row) vs PlainConstructs.tid (20 keys): intersection 0, split by fault right, by storage arbitrary (YIN A2). theme-paint / family-atlas / page-palette / reading-recipe — HELD (F.11). uri-span-measure/compare — PROTECTED. Theme population: node_modules/tm-themes hardcoded in five instruments; 65; `BAR = 16` literal vs \"a quarter of 65\" prose (YIN R4)